1
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:51,370
片名：致命魔术

2
00:00:57,550 --> 00:00:58,880
Are you watching closely?

3
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:10,190
Every magic trick consists of three parts, or acts.

4
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:13,670
The first part is called "the pledge."

5
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,100
The magician shows you something ordinary.

6
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:20,630
A deck of cards, a bird or a man.

7
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:29,680
He shows you this object.

8
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:32,350
Perhaps he asks you to inspect it,

9
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,450
to see that it is indeed real,

10
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,580
unaltered, normal.

11
00:01:42,230 --> 00:01:45,130
But, of course, it probably isn't.

12
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,000
Where do you think you're going?

13
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:49,430
I'm part of the bloody act, you fool.

14
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:04,820
The second act is called "the turn."

15
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:22,330
The magician takes the ordinary something

16
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:27,000
and makes it do something extraordinary.

17
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:35,610
Now you're looking for the secret, but you won't find it,

18
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,520
because, of course, you're not really looking.

19
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:41,210
You don't really want to know.

20
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,850
You want to be... fooled.

21
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:50,960
But you wouldn't clap yet,

22
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:55,200
because making something disappear isn't enough.

23
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:58,870
You have to bring it back.

24
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:04,140
That's why every magic trick has a third act.

25
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:05,870
The hardest part.

26
00:03:05,950 --> 00:03:08,470
The part we call...

27
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:09,880
..."the prestige."

28
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:24,860
The prestige? And did Robert Angier, the Great Danton, your employer,

29
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:27,370
get to that final part of his trick that night?

30
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:29,530
No, sir. Something went wrong. What went wrong?

31
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:34,200
I saw someone making their way below stage.

32
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:36,180
I followed him.

33
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:39,510
It was Borden, watching Mr. Angier drown.

34
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,020
Would you please describe your occupation to the jury, Mr. Cutter.

35
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:45,450
I'm an ingenieur, I design illusions

36
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,680
and construct the apparatus necessary for performing them.

37
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:53,190
So, Mr. Cutter, was this water-filled tank beneath the stage

38
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:54,850
part of Mr. Angier's illusion?

39
00:03:54,930 --> 00:03:58,800
No. The tank was brought on for the first trick

40
00:03:58,870 --> 00:04:00,830
and then taken offstage.

41
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,200
Borden must have placed it under the trap door after the end of it.

42
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:05,830
How large was this tank?

43
00:04:05,910 --> 00:04:08,500
It's a normal tank for escape under water,

44
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:10,700
about 400 or 500 gallons.

45
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:13,270
How do you think he was able to move the tank

46
00:04:13,350 --> 00:04:15,710
under the trap door without anyone noticing?

47
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:18,150
He's a magician, ask him.

48
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:19,310
I ask again,

49
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:22,620
would this man explain the mechanics of Mr. Angier's illusion.

50
00:04:22,690 --> 00:04:26,020
The Real Transported Man is one of the most sought after illusions

51
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:27,290
in this business.

52
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:28,920
I have the right to sell it on.

53
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,630
If I reveal the method here, the trick is won'thless.

54
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:33,960
How can we know that the tank wasn't simply

55
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,500
some part of the trick that went wrong?

56
00:04:36,570 --> 00:04:38,630
Mr. Cutter, I see your predicament.

57
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:41,110
But Alfred Borders life hangs in the balance.

58
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:44,340
If you were prepared to disclose the details to me in private,

59
00:04:44,410 --> 00:04:47,250
I might be able to judge their relevance to the case.

60
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:49,940
Might this be an acceptable compromise?

61
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:11,600
I'm going to have to ask you to turn out your pockets.

62
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:19,300
Not my idea, sir. The warden saw his show in Manchester last year

63
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,250
where he vanished into thin air.

64
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:23,810
He's convinced he'll try and escape.

65
00:05:27,220 --> 00:05:30,020
I told him the only way Borders going to disappear

66
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:32,530
is if I leave him out there with the other inmates.

67
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:39,730
Check the locks...

68
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:42,990
...twice.

69
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:52,600
My name is Owens.

70
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:54,940
I'm a solicitor.

71
00:05:55,020 --> 00:05:58,680
I represent Lord Caldlow, an accomplished amateur magician and...

72
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,350
How much?

73
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:03,750
He is interested... How much for my tricks?

74
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:05,420
Five thousand pounds.

75
00:06:05,490 --> 00:06:07,980
Talk to Fallon, my ingenieur. Money's for him.

76
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:11,760
Yes, I did. He offered to sell me your tricks.

77
00:06:11,830 --> 00:06:14,700
All except the most valuable one, The Transported Man.

78
00:06:14,770 --> 00:06:18,070
Well, I won't forgive myself for selling my greatest trick.

79
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,570
Even for your daughter?

80
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:25,580
If you are for the drop, your daughter is going to need looking after.

81
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:27,380
Fallon will take care of her.

82
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:29,180
Perfect, Fallon.

83
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:31,280
A man with a past as obscure as your own.

84
00:06:31,350 --> 00:06:36,380
The courts have motioned she be removed from his care. She will be an orphan.

85
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:38,720
I know you're no stranger to the workhouse.

86
00:06:39,490 --> 00:06:41,330
It's better than here?

87
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:44,630
I'm offering you a way to wrap up your affairs with dignity.

88
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:46,670
I'm offering your daughter a future.

89
00:06:46,730 --> 00:06:49,760
As Lord Caldlow's ward, she will want for nothing...

90
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:51,670
...ever.

91
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:54,730
Well, think it over.

92
00:06:54,810 --> 00:06:58,580
And Lord Caldlow would like you to have this,

93
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:01,170
as a show of good faith. It might be of interest.

94
00:07:01,250 --> 00:07:04,780
Robert Angier's diary, including the time he spent in Colorado

95
00:07:04,850 --> 00:07:06,110
learning your trick.

96
00:07:06,190 --> 00:07:07,880
He never learned it. Really?

97
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:11,480
On his return, he mounted a version of The Transported Man

98
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:15,520
that the papers said was better even than your original.

99
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,790
If you want Angier's secrets,

100
00:07:17,870 --> 00:07:22,430
you go dig him up and ask him yourself.

101
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,370
I want your secret, Mr. Borden.

102
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:28,740
Consider your daughter.

103
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:38,150
A cipher,

104
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:40,240
an enigma...

105
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:43,990
...a search,

106
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:45,050
a search for answers.

107
00:07:52,730 --> 00:07:56,530
Even if Colorado is the end of my journey,

108
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,800
it'll take much longer to unravel

109
00:07:59,870 --> 00:08:01,770
the rest of Borders secrets.

110
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:05,810
See, the cipher in his notebook is

111
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:08,370
unlocked by a single word,

112
00:08:08,450 --> 00:08:11,420
but it will still take months to translate his writing.

113
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:14,250
And to know his mind.

114
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:19,520
Well, my passion is equal to the task.

115
00:08:32,610 --> 00:08:36,130
Mr. Angier, welcome to Colorado Springs.

116
00:08:51,530 --> 00:08:54,590
The whole town has electricity. Yes, sir.

117
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:13,270
Well...

118
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:14,840
...quite a reception.

119
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,410
You're our first guest of the season, Mr. Angier.

120
00:09:17,490 --> 00:09:20,980
Your telegram didn't indicate how long you'd be staying with us.

121
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:22,680
As long as it takes.

122
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,020
I will need a coach tomorrow to take me up the mountain.

123
00:09:26,090 --> 00:09:27,820
Well, the peak's closed, sir.

124
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:29,730
For scientific experimentation.

125
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,770
Yes, I know.

126
00:09:31,830 --> 00:09:33,130
That's why I'm here.

127
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:47,250
You'll have to walk the rest, I'm afraid, sir.

128
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:26,010
I'm amazed how many of you newspaper writers

129
00:10:26,090 --> 00:10:28,150
can't read my sign.

130
00:10:29,150 --> 00:10:31,150
电网

131
00:10:32,890 --> 00:10:35,490
Not the welcome I was expecting.

132
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:40,530
I know you.

133
00:10:44,510 --> 00:10:46,030
You're the Great Danton.

134
00:10:46,110 --> 00:10:48,410
I saw your show in London.

135
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:51,240
Seven times you guessed every object

136
00:10:51,310 --> 00:10:53,540
the audience had in their pockets.

137
00:10:53,610 --> 00:10:57,680
I'm Alley. Sorry about the fence. People keep interfering with our work.

138
00:10:57,750 --> 00:11:00,410
I've come to see Tesla. Why?

139
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:04,550
He made a machine for a colleague of mine some time ago.

140
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:08,120
Can you get me a meeting with him? Impossible, I'm afraid.

141
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,430
I've brought a lot of money.

142
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:12,160
I'm sorry, Mr. Angier.

143
00:11:13,230 --> 00:11:15,790
I simply can't help.

144
00:11:16,970 --> 00:11:20,100
I'll be staying at the hotel, indefinitely.

145
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,540
Hey, what am I holding?

146
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:26,910
Your watch.

147
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:35,210
Borders journal entry for April 3, 1897,

148
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:37,380
describes a show at the Orpheum Theatre.

149
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:40,360
That was just days after he first met me.

150
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:46,460
We were two young men at the start of a great career,

151
00:11:46,530 --> 00:11:48,870
two young men devoted to an illusion,

152
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:51,500
two young men who never intended to hurt anyone.

153
00:11:51,570 --> 00:11:53,340
Which of you brave souls

154
00:11:53,410 --> 00:11:56,140
is willing to bind this lovely young woman?

155
00:11:57,910 --> 00:11:59,850
Me!

156
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:09,850
If you would tie her wrists,

157
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:12,820
bind her feet...

158
00:12:12,890 --> 00:12:15,420
...around the ankle.

159
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,590
Are either of you two gentlemen sailors?

160
00:12:17,670 --> 00:12:19,500
No. No.

161
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:23,400
I'm sure you can both tie a strong knot.

162
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:30,100
He's complacent, he's predictable, he's boring.

163
00:13:30,170 --> 00:13:32,730
Milton has gotten success, whatever that means,

164
00:13:32,810 --> 00:13:36,040
so now he's scared. He won't take any risks at all.

165
00:13:36,110 --> 00:13:39,170
He's squandering the goodwill of his audience with tired,

166
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:41,840
second-rate tricks... They're all favorites.

167
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:45,410
Give me something fresh. He won't even try a bloody bullet catch.

168
00:13:45,490 --> 00:13:47,320
A bullet catch is suicide.

169
00:13:47,390 --> 00:13:51,380
All it takes is some smartass volunteer to put a button in the barrel.

170
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:53,720
Use a plant. You can't for every trick.

171
00:13:53,790 --> 00:13:55,760
No seats left for the punters.

172
00:13:55,830 --> 00:13:59,630
All right, no bullet catch, whatever, but the point is a real magician

173
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:01,330
tries to invent something new

174
00:14:01,400 --> 00:14:04,270
that other magicians will scratch their heads over.

175
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:06,530
Then you sell it to him for a small fortune.

176
00:14:06,610 --> 00:14:09,700
What? I suppose you have such a trick?

177
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:12,340
I sure do. Would you care to sell it to me?

178
00:14:12,410 --> 00:14:14,010
No. No one else can do my trick.

179
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:16,210
Any trick can be duplicated. Wrong.

180
00:14:16,280 --> 00:14:20,010
If Mr. Borden has invented his masterpiece,

181
00:14:20,090 --> 00:14:21,990
he's prepared to do it.

182
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:24,180
Milton is a showman, but Borden is right,

183
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:25,890
he won't get his hands dirty.

184
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:29,520
If you want to see what it takes to make real magic,

185
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:33,430
go to the Tenley. There's a Chinaman there and he really has what it takes.

186
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:35,730
Chung Ling Soo. I can't afford that.

187
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:37,530
I know the bloke run the door.

188
00:14:37,610 --> 00:14:41,940
You two go and see that show, and whichever one of you can tell me

189
00:14:42,010 --> 00:14:45,100
how he does the goldfish bowl trick gets the prize.

190
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:47,440
Which is? Ten minutes onstage

191
00:14:47,510 --> 00:14:49,350
with my old friend, Mr. Ackerman.

192
00:14:49,420 --> 00:14:50,850
Really? Who's Ackerman?

193
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:53,980
The top theatrical agent in London.

194
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:57,050
I saw you drop the knot again. I turned my wrists.

195
00:14:57,120 --> 00:14:59,750
Some nights you just don't get it, do you?

196
00:14:59,830 --> 00:15:02,690
I mean, if that knot slips and Julia's on the hoist,

197
00:15:02,760 --> 00:15:03,860
she'll break her leg.

198
00:15:03,930 --> 00:15:05,560
It's the wrong knot.

199
00:15:05,630 --> 00:15:08,500
Like I said, the Langford double will hold tighter.

200
00:15:08,570 --> 00:15:12,100
The Langford double isn't a wet knot. It's too dangerous.

201
00:15:12,170 --> 00:15:14,400
If the rope swells, she can't slip it.

202
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:17,710
I can slip a Langford underwater. We can practice.

203
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:21,710
Borden, he said no. Oh, you know knots better than me?

204
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:23,770
Listen, no more mistakes.

205
00:15:23,850 --> 00:15:26,550
All right. Yeah? Do you? You want to take over?

206
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:29,560
Just leave. I didn't think so.

207
00:15:33,990 --> 00:15:35,830
Hey, Cutter, where's he from?

208
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:39,200
Where are you from? He shifts props for Virgil at the hall.

209
00:15:39,270 --> 00:15:42,430
Worried he'll steal your tricks? Doesn't deal in methods.

210
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:45,560
How do you know? Because I hired him

211
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,870
to find out how Virgil does the orange trick.

212
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:51,280
I don't trust him. He's a natural magician.

213
00:15:51,350 --> 00:15:53,440
'Course you can't trust him.

214
00:15:53,510 --> 00:15:54,880
I think he's all right.

215
00:15:54,950 --> 00:15:57,280
You think everyone's all right. Even you.

216
00:15:57,350 --> 00:16:01,190
Watch your sightlines. If I can see you kiss your wife's leg every night,

217
00:16:01,260 --> 00:16:03,920
so can the blokes at the ends of rows three and four!

218
00:16:35,990 --> 00:16:38,420
You're wrong. It can't be.

219
00:16:38,490 --> 00:16:42,220
Look at the man. This is the trick.

220
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:44,320
This is the performance. Right here.

221
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:46,760
This is why no one can detect his method.

222
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:50,130
Total devotion to his art.

223
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:52,870
A lot of self-sacrifice.

224
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:55,310
You know.

225
00:16:55,380 --> 00:16:58,070
It's the only way to escape...

226
00:16:58,150 --> 00:17:00,580
...all this, you know?

227
00:17:04,350 --> 00:17:08,050
I can barely lift this thing and it's not even filled with water.

228
00:17:09,690 --> 00:17:12,020
Or fish, look.

229
00:17:12,090 --> 00:17:14,360
I don't know. Hang on a second.

230
00:17:16,300 --> 00:17:18,990
He must be strong as an ox.

231
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:24,470
He's been pretending to be a cripple for years.

232
00:17:24,540 --> 00:17:28,130
Any time he's in public, any time he goes out. It's unthinkable.

233
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:31,140
Borden saw it at once. But I couldn't fathom it.

234
00:17:31,210 --> 00:17:34,080
Living my whole life pretending to be someone else.

235
00:17:34,150 --> 00:17:35,880
You are pretending.

236
00:17:35,950 --> 00:17:37,970
I don't think changing a name compares.

237
00:17:38,050 --> 00:17:40,520
Not your name. Who you are and where you're from.

238
00:17:40,590 --> 00:17:44,550
I promised I wouldn't embarrass my family with my theatrical endeavors.

239
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:48,320
I thought of a name for you.

240
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:53,190
The Great Danton.

241
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:56,240
Do you like it? But it's sophisticated.

242
00:17:56,300 --> 00:17:57,740
It's French.

243
00:18:03,710 --> 00:18:07,870
Borden writes as if no one but he understood the true nature of magic.

244
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:16,250
But what does he know of self-sacrifice?

245
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,490
You bloody fool.

246
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:54,650
He killed it. It's OK.

247
00:18:54,730 --> 00:18:58,560
He killed it. He didn't.

248
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,330
Look, now he'll bring it back.

249
00:19:06,570 --> 00:19:09,510
No, he killed it. No, he didn't.

250
00:19:17,820 --> 00:19:20,790
Look, see, he's all right. He's fine.

251
00:19:20,850 --> 00:19:24,950
Look at him. But where's his brother?

252
00:19:27,660 --> 00:19:30,930
He's a sharp lad, your son.

253
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,590
Oh, he's my nephew.

254
00:19:39,410 --> 00:19:40,900
You're the lucky one today.

255
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:04,660
Are you watching closely?

256
00:20:07,430 --> 00:20:08,920
Look closer.

257
00:20:12,010 --> 00:20:13,800
Never show anyone.

258
00:20:13,870 --> 00:20:16,600
They'll beg you and flatter you for the secret,

259
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:20,170
but as soon as you give it up, you'll be nothing to them.

260
00:20:20,250 --> 00:20:22,510
You understand? Nothing.

261
00:20:22,580 --> 00:20:25,640
The secret impresses no one.

262
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:29,310
The trick you use it for is everything.

263
00:20:33,560 --> 00:20:36,500
Well, thank you for lunch, Mr. Borden.

264
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:38,050
You're welcome.

265
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:41,160
Alfred, it's Alfred.

266
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:42,830
Alfred.

267
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:45,870
So I could use a cup of tea.

268
00:20:45,940 --> 00:20:49,470
I can't allow... It's the landlord. It's not...

269
00:20:50,910 --> 00:20:54,140
You think that's... Is that enough to keep me out?

270
00:20:54,210 --> 00:20:57,450
I think so.

271
00:20:58,450 --> 00:21:00,040
So I'll see you again?

272
00:21:09,260 --> 00:21:11,750
Milk and sugar?

273
00:21:32,850 --> 00:21:34,820
What's in there?

274
00:21:34,890 --> 00:21:36,820
Angier's machine.

275
00:21:45,400 --> 00:21:47,030
You built this, Mr. Cutter?

276
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:49,970
Oh, no, sir. This wasn't built by a magician.

277
00:21:50,040 --> 00:21:52,060
This was built by a wizard.

278
00:21:52,140 --> 00:21:57,130
A man who can actually do what magicians pretend to do.

279
00:21:57,210 --> 00:22:01,410
Tell me, Your Honor, what happens with these things after the trial?

280
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:03,450
They've been sold to a Lord Caldlow.

281
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:06,280
An avid collector, very interested in the case.

282
00:22:06,350 --> 00:22:08,840
Yeah. Well, don't let him take this.

283
00:22:08,920 --> 00:22:11,720
Why ever not? It's too dangerous.

284
00:22:11,790 --> 00:22:13,950
I'm sure beneath its bells and whistles

285
00:22:14,030 --> 00:22:16,330
it's got a simple and disappointing trick.

286
00:22:16,400 --> 00:22:18,830
The most disappointing of all, sir.

287
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:22,700
It has no trick.

288
00:22:25,140 --> 00:22:26,730
It's real.

289
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:32,140
This is the tank Angier drowned in?

290
00:22:32,210 --> 00:22:34,270
Yes.

291
00:22:34,350 --> 00:22:36,510
This is the...

292
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:39,640
...place where the performer's hand reaches through

293
00:22:39,720 --> 00:22:40,980
to the trick padlock.

294
00:22:41,050 --> 00:22:43,320
A standard magical apparatus for escapes?

295
00:22:43,390 --> 00:22:45,880
Yeah, with one important difference.

296
00:22:45,960 --> 00:22:47,980
This isn't a trick lock.

297
00:22:48,060 --> 00:22:50,030
It's been switched for a real one.

298
00:22:50,100 --> 00:22:52,070
What a way to kill someone.

299
00:22:52,130 --> 00:22:54,830
They're magicians, Your Honor.

300
00:22:54,900 --> 00:23:00,310
Showmen, men who live by dressing up plain and sometimes brutal truths,

301
00:23:00,710 --> 00:23:03,470
to amaze, to shock.

302
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:05,700
Even without an audience?

303
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:08,850
There was an audience.

304
00:23:09,680 --> 00:23:11,740
You see, this water tank

305
00:23:11,820 --> 00:23:15,250
was of particular significance to these two men.

306
00:23:16,560 --> 00:23:19,080
Particularly awful significance.

307
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:26,990
Which of you brave souls is willing to bind this lovely young woman?

308
00:23:29,570 --> 00:23:31,060
If you would tie her wrists,

309
00:23:31,140 --> 00:23:33,300
bind her feet around the ankle.

310
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:49,110
Are either of you gentlemen sailors? No.

311
00:23:51,090 --> 00:23:53,290
I'm sure you can both tie a strong knot.

312
00:24:48,480 --> 00:24:49,680
Robert!

313
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:13,240
No, no.

314
00:25:17,210 --> 00:25:19,740
Julia. Julia!

315
00:26:04,420 --> 00:26:07,290
I knew an old sailor once.

316
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:10,260
They told me he went overboard,

317
00:26:10,330 --> 00:26:12,420
tangled in the sails.

318
00:26:12,500 --> 00:26:14,300
They pulled him out,

319
00:26:14,370 --> 00:26:18,270
but it took him five minutes to cough.

320
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:20,360
He said it was like...

321
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:22,270
...going home.

322
00:26:27,610 --> 00:26:29,950
What do you want, Borden?

323
00:26:36,760 --> 00:26:38,780
I'm sorry for your loss, Angier.

324
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:44,660
Which knot did you tie?

325
00:26:47,800 --> 00:26:50,740
I keep asking myself that.

326
00:26:50,800 --> 00:26:52,800
And? And...

327
00:26:54,540 --> 00:26:57,410
I'm sorry, I just don't know.

328
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:00,970
You don't know?

329
00:27:02,820 --> 00:27:04,750
I'm sorry.

330
00:27:05,720 --> 00:27:08,310
You don't know?

331
00:27:11,930 --> 00:27:14,120
You don't know?!

332
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:23,730
Alfred? Good news. We got our first booking.

333
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:26,140
I don't think we've had the pleasure.

334
00:27:26,210 --> 00:27:29,230
Mr. Fallon is my ingenieur. Where did you get all?

335
00:27:29,310 --> 00:27:33,300
I had to borrow and... don't ask. He's a very enterprising soul

336
00:27:33,380 --> 00:27:35,180
is... is Mr. Fallon. So...

337
00:27:35,250 --> 00:27:38,680
We can't afford to pay him. The money will come with the audience.

338
00:27:38,750 --> 00:27:42,450
But what about until then? What I earn, it's barely enough for us.

339
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:46,480
Don't worry about it. I'll share half my food with him or something.

340
00:27:46,560 --> 00:27:49,530
You're going to be doing that with someone else.

341
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:51,500
No. You're...

342
00:27:51,560 --> 00:27:53,830
Having a baby.

343
00:27:53,900 --> 00:27:56,800
Oh, my God.

344
00:27:56,870 --> 00:27:59,700
We should have told Fallon. That is great.

345
00:27:59,770 --> 00:28:01,010
Oh, come here.

346
00:28:05,350 --> 00:28:06,870
Alfred, what is this?

347
00:28:06,950 --> 00:28:11,110
Oh, this is just the trick to work Ackerman up at the end of my act.

348
00:28:11,180 --> 00:28:13,880
Is it the masterpiece, the great trick?

349
00:28:13,950 --> 00:28:16,720
No, no, no, the world is not ready for that one yet.

350
00:28:16,790 --> 00:28:21,120
This is just your run-of-the-mill, daring, spectacular bullet catch.

351
00:28:21,190 --> 00:28:23,720
A bullet catch. Yeah, but it's safe.

352
00:28:23,800 --> 00:28:26,100
I promise. Look.

353
00:28:28,470 --> 00:28:30,730
Shoot me. Shoot you?

354
00:28:30,800 --> 00:28:32,030
Go on, shoot me.

355
00:28:34,540 --> 00:28:36,570
Right there. No, I can't.

356
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:39,200
No, no, not there. Shoot me here. Don't...

357
00:28:43,450 --> 00:28:46,320
How's that?

358
00:28:46,390 --> 00:28:49,720
That's very good. Tell me how you do it.

359
00:28:49,790 --> 00:28:50,920
No, I can't do that.

360
00:28:52,990 --> 00:28:55,860
Well, then you can't do it. I can't do it?

361
00:28:55,930 --> 00:28:59,260
I'm sorry, but I can't raise a child on my own.

362
00:28:59,330 --> 00:29:01,660
You don't tell anybody that I told you.

363
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:04,070
OK. Gunpowder.

364
00:29:06,640 --> 00:29:07,770
Wadding.

365
00:29:09,410 --> 00:29:11,500
Then the bullet.

366
00:29:12,110 --> 00:29:13,840
Ramrod.

367
00:29:18,550 --> 00:29:20,540
Hold out your hand.

368
00:29:22,620 --> 00:29:26,560
The bullet is not even in the gun when the charge is fired.

369
00:29:26,630 --> 00:29:30,390
Once you know, it's actually very obvious.

370
00:29:31,460 --> 00:29:33,460
Well, I mean, you still get...

371
00:29:33,530 --> 00:29:35,130
It's dangerous.

372
00:29:35,200 --> 00:29:37,400
People still get killed doing that. How?

373
00:29:37,470 --> 00:29:40,200
You get some smartass who could put a penny or a button

374
00:29:40,270 --> 00:29:43,610
or, God forbid, Sarah, somebody could put a bullet down there.

375
00:29:43,680 --> 00:29:46,870
Don't worry. Don't worry,

376
00:29:46,950 --> 00:29:50,210
because I'm not gonna let anything happen.

377
00:29:50,280 --> 00:29:53,150
Everything's gonna be all right because...

378
00:29:54,520 --> 00:29:57,790
...I love you very much.

379
00:30:00,430 --> 00:30:03,730
Say it again. I love you.

380
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:07,270
Not today. What?

381
00:30:07,330 --> 00:30:10,500
Well, some days it's not true, and today you don't mean it.

382
00:30:10,570 --> 00:30:13,800
Maybe today you're more in love with magic than me.

383
00:30:13,870 --> 00:30:16,500
I like being able to tell the difference.

384
00:30:16,580 --> 00:30:19,370
It makes the days it is true

385
00:30:19,450 --> 00:30:21,780
mean something.

386
00:30:23,420 --> 00:30:24,850
All right.

387
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:47,140
Metal rings, ladies and gentlemen.

388
00:30:47,210 --> 00:30:49,870
If there are any ladies and gentlemen here.

389
00:30:49,940 --> 00:30:53,310
Solid metal. Seen that already.

390
00:31:03,960 --> 00:31:05,050
Who threw that?

391
00:31:21,640 --> 00:31:23,440
This is what you came for, is it?

392
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:28,770
So...

393
00:31:29,880 --> 00:31:31,780
...who wants to volunteer?

394
00:31:31,850 --> 00:31:33,610
I do! Me!

395
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:42,420
Me.

396
00:31:44,200 --> 00:31:46,260
Me.

397
00:31:59,680 --> 00:32:02,170
Are you man enough, sir?

398
00:32:07,150 --> 00:32:08,980
Yes.

399
00:32:19,770 --> 00:32:21,830
Which knot did you tie, Borden?

400
00:32:28,040 --> 00:32:30,310
Which knot did you tie, Borden?

401
00:32:31,480 --> 00:32:33,170
I don't know.

402
00:32:48,730 --> 00:32:52,390
He came in to demand an answer, and I told him the truth.

403
00:32:52,470 --> 00:32:55,630
That I have fought with myself over that night,

404
00:32:55,700 --> 00:32:59,040
one half of me swearing blind that I tied a simple slipknot,

405
00:32:59,110 --> 00:33:02,470
the other half convinced that I tied the Langford double.

406
00:33:02,540 --> 00:33:04,570
I can never know for sure.

407
00:33:07,510 --> 00:33:09,780
How can he not know!

408
00:33:09,850 --> 00:33:11,610
How can he not know?

409
00:33:13,250 --> 00:33:15,740
He must know what he did.

410
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:17,220
He must.

411
00:33:19,860 --> 00:33:21,850
Sarah, that bloody hurts.

412
00:33:22,560 --> 00:33:25,190
I don't understand how it can be bleeding again.

413
00:33:25,260 --> 00:33:27,700
It's as bad as the day it happened.

414
00:33:29,440 --> 00:33:33,370
We have to get a doctor back. We can't afford the doctor back.

415
00:33:34,940 --> 00:33:38,540
You've woken her. Great.

416
00:33:38,610 --> 00:33:41,510
I'm sorry, I need this to heal so I can get back to work.

417
00:33:41,580 --> 00:33:45,350
You've got to face things. What tricks can you perform with this injury?

418
00:33:45,420 --> 00:33:48,880
I can do car pulls. I can do some prop tricks.

419
00:33:48,960 --> 00:33:53,320
I can still do that. I can do the trick I've been telling you about.

420
00:33:53,390 --> 00:33:55,520
The one that they're gonna remember me for.

421
00:33:57,900 --> 00:33:59,490
Come on.

422
00:34:25,060 --> 00:34:28,260
I never thought I'd find an answer at the bottom of a pint glass.

423
00:34:28,330 --> 00:34:29,920
Hasn't stopped your looking.

424
00:34:35,130 --> 00:34:38,000
Heard about a booking. Nice little theater.

425
00:34:38,070 --> 00:34:40,900
Young, up-and-coming magician. Who?

426
00:34:40,970 --> 00:34:43,700
You. Me? Booking? Why?

427
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:45,870
I want to keep on working.

428
00:34:45,950 --> 00:34:49,180
Who's going to hire the ingenieur that killed Julia McCullough

429
00:34:49,250 --> 00:34:51,380
in front of a sellout crowd at the Orpheum?

430
00:34:52,150 --> 00:34:54,350
Someone who knows it wasn't your fault.

431
00:34:54,420 --> 00:34:57,910
Someone who knows Alfred Borden and his repertoire of exotic knots.

432
00:34:57,990 --> 00:35:01,520
I hear he had a spot of bad luck catching a bullet south of the river.

433
00:35:01,590 --> 00:35:04,390
That's a dangerous trick, that one.

434
00:35:09,600 --> 00:35:11,570
We'll have to whitewash the windows,

435
00:35:11,640 --> 00:35:15,330
confound the more curious members of the public, but it will do.

436
00:35:17,180 --> 00:35:20,810
We should see about an assistant. I've made some arrangements.

437
00:35:21,710 --> 00:35:23,480
You settled on a name?

438
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,040
Yes, I have.

439
00:35:26,120 --> 00:35:27,980
The Great Danton.

440
00:35:28,050 --> 00:35:30,580
It's a bit old-fashioned, isn't it?

441
00:35:31,990 --> 00:35:34,220
No, it's sophisticated.

442
00:35:34,290 --> 00:35:37,060
The birdcage can't be our climax. Everybody knows it.

443
00:35:37,130 --> 00:35:39,530
Not like this. I don't want to kill doves.

444
00:35:39,600 --> 00:35:41,460
Then stay off the stage.

445
00:35:41,530 --> 00:35:43,230
You're a magician, not a wizard.

446
00:35:43,300 --> 00:35:47,170
You got to get your hands dirty, if you're going to achieve the impossible.

447
00:35:48,640 --> 00:35:51,010
In here, Miss Wenscombe.

448
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:55,540
No point in meeting Mr. Angier if you don't fit. On the right.

449
00:35:58,720 --> 00:36:00,740
What's so hard about this? Nothing.

450
00:36:00,820 --> 00:36:03,350
You're going... down here.

451
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:12,030
And this will conceal, right? Oh, sure.

452
00:36:12,700 --> 00:36:14,290
Tighten up on there. Yep.

453
00:36:14,370 --> 00:36:15,390
OK.

454
00:36:17,500 --> 00:36:18,560
This ties up here?

455
00:36:18,640 --> 00:36:20,040
Ties in the back. Good.

456
00:36:26,110 --> 00:36:28,310
Breathe as little as possible.

457
00:36:34,820 --> 00:36:37,450
Now, you see?

458
00:36:37,520 --> 00:36:38,920
That's it. OK.

459
00:36:42,360 --> 00:36:44,190
She's not experienced,

460
00:36:44,260 --> 00:36:46,290
but she knows how to present herself.

461
00:36:46,370 --> 00:36:50,960
A pretty assistant is the most effective form of misdirection.

462
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:54,400
Thank you. Ladies and gentlemen, for my final trick,

463
00:36:54,470 --> 00:36:57,240
I will require the assistance of two volunteers.

464
00:36:57,310 --> 00:36:59,570
Mr. Merrit, if you would oblige me?

465
00:37:01,480 --> 00:37:03,000
Yep.

466
00:37:04,820 --> 00:37:06,510
Under? Under, yeah.

467
00:37:08,090 --> 00:37:09,380
Come around his foot.

468
00:37:09,460 --> 00:37:10,890
Careful. It's all right.

469
00:37:31,740 --> 00:37:34,010
Your volunteer, put their hand there. Yeah?

470
00:37:34,080 --> 00:37:35,910
And another one here.

471
00:37:35,980 --> 00:37:39,510
If you'll place your hands on either side of the cage, please.

472
00:37:39,590 --> 00:37:40,920
Thank you, Olivia.

473
00:37:40,990 --> 00:37:44,750
You best not be intending to hurt this animal, Mr. Angier.

474
00:37:44,820 --> 00:37:47,190
Of course not. Ready? Right.

475
00:37:47,960 --> 00:37:50,760
One... Two...

476
00:37:51,500 --> 00:37:52,760
Three.

477
00:37:56,770 --> 00:37:58,570
That's bloody marvelous, Cutter.

478
00:37:59,570 --> 00:38:00,770
Very nice.

479
00:38:00,840 --> 00:38:02,640
And the best part is...

480
00:38:08,010 --> 00:38:10,450
I thought you said I had to get my hands dirty.

481
00:38:10,520 --> 00:38:12,420
Someday, perhaps you will.

482
00:38:12,490 --> 00:38:14,040
I just had to know that you can.

483
00:38:14,120 --> 00:38:16,050
Very nice. Very nice, indeed.

484
00:38:16,120 --> 00:38:19,790
I haven't had the chance to compliment you on your beautiful theater.

485
00:38:19,860 --> 00:38:22,230
It'll be a lot more beautiful when it's full.

486
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:23,280
Don't worry.

487
00:38:23,360 --> 00:38:25,590
You all say that. Why should I worry?

488
00:38:25,670 --> 00:38:28,530
If your tricks don't get them in, somebody else's will.

489
00:38:28,600 --> 00:38:32,700
Maybe somebody willing to do a bullet catch or a water escape?

490
00:38:32,770 --> 00:38:34,330
Cheap thrills, Mr. Merrit.

491
00:38:34,410 --> 00:38:38,070
People hoping for an accident, likely to see one too.

492
00:38:38,140 --> 00:38:40,140
What would that do for your business?

493
00:38:40,210 --> 00:38:43,080
You got a week, John.

494
00:38:43,150 --> 00:38:46,520
Thank you, Mr. Merrit.

495
00:38:54,490 --> 00:38:55,860
Thank you.

496
00:38:57,660 --> 00:39:01,290
Uh... Sir, in the third row there.

497
00:39:01,370 --> 00:39:04,060
Please stand up and show us your handkerchief.

498
00:39:06,070 --> 00:39:07,870
This isn't mine.

499
00:39:07,940 --> 00:39:11,400
Perhaps you'd return it to the lady in the aisle of the second row.

500
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:13,780
I believe she has yours.

501
00:39:20,120 --> 00:39:24,780
I'm sorry. I'm so sorry. I'm making so many mistakes. I'm so nervous.

502
00:39:24,860 --> 00:39:27,120
The audience doesn't seem to be responding.

503
00:39:27,190 --> 00:39:29,420
They've seen a lot of tricks before,

504
00:39:29,500 --> 00:39:31,990
but not this next one.

505
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:42,800
Cloth over.

506
00:39:42,880 --> 00:39:44,640
Keep your fingers crossed.

507
00:39:44,710 --> 00:39:46,970
I'll have the champagne ready.

508
00:40:01,460 --> 00:40:04,760
You've seen this one before? We've seen them all before.

509
00:40:04,830 --> 00:40:06,960
I'll make it a little harder then, shall I?

510
00:40:07,030 --> 00:40:10,000
Two volunteers, please. A lady and a gentlemen.

511
00:40:10,070 --> 00:40:13,270
To hold this cage with me.

512
00:40:13,340 --> 00:40:15,310
I'll perform this feat in a manner

513
00:40:15,370 --> 00:40:19,900
never before seen by yourselves or any other audience anywhere in the world.

514
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:25,010
Madame, place one hand on the back of the cage,

515
00:40:25,080 --> 00:40:26,610
one hand on the front.

516
00:40:26,690 --> 00:40:29,050
Sir, one hand on the bottom of the cage

517
00:40:29,120 --> 00:40:30,650
one hand on the top.

518
00:40:44,140 --> 00:40:47,040
I should've spotted him. You had a lot of plates going.

519
00:40:47,110 --> 00:40:49,270
Don't suppose we can do this again. No.

520
00:40:49,340 --> 00:40:51,780
So, what's the climax of our show? Show?

521
00:40:51,850 --> 00:40:54,970
You don't have a show. We have a week's engagement.

522
00:40:55,050 --> 00:40:58,640
To perform magic, not butcher birds and break my customers'fingers.

523
00:40:58,720 --> 00:41:01,410
Clear out, anything here in the morning gets burned.

524
00:41:01,490 --> 00:41:03,920
Mr. Merrit... It's done, John.

525
00:41:03,990 --> 00:41:06,790
I've hired a comedian. You know I hate comedians.

526
00:41:08,930 --> 00:41:11,420
Well, there's plenty of good theaters.

527
00:41:11,500 --> 00:41:14,330
If we can come up with a new trick,

528
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:17,630
change the name of the act. The name stays.

529
00:41:19,470 --> 00:41:24,600
Right, well, then the new trick's gonna have to be irresistible then.

530
00:41:24,680 --> 00:41:26,700
I have a couple of methods to try out.

531
00:41:26,780 --> 00:41:30,010
And then we need a new angle on the presentation.

532
00:41:30,080 --> 00:41:31,910
And if you need some inspiration,

533
00:41:31,990 --> 00:41:35,150
there's a technical exposition at the Albert Hall this week.

534
00:41:35,220 --> 00:41:37,950
Engineers, scientists, you know?

535
00:41:38,020 --> 00:41:41,320
That sort of thing captures the public's imagination.

536
00:41:58,380 --> 00:42:01,780
Mind if I join you? Tesla sends me down here during the storms.

537
00:42:01,850 --> 00:42:05,080
Perfect excuse to come join the Great Danton for a drink.

538
00:42:06,050 --> 00:42:08,450
Two of them.

539
00:42:08,520 --> 00:42:11,150
Beautiful, isn't it? God, I miss New York, though.

540
00:42:11,220 --> 00:42:13,660
Why are you here? The lightning lives here.

541
00:42:13,730 --> 00:42:15,350
And not much else.

542
00:42:15,430 --> 00:42:16,950
Our work is secret.

543
00:42:17,600 --> 00:42:19,500
That a cipher?

544
00:42:21,600 --> 00:42:24,730
A rotating transposition that shifts every day of the diary.

545
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:28,800
Simple but time-consuming to translate, even with the five-letter keyword.

546
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:30,600
Which is?

547
00:42:30,680 --> 00:42:32,670
We magicians have a circle of trust.

548
00:42:32,750 --> 00:42:36,840
You have a circle of trust with someone whose diary you stole?

549
00:42:36,920 --> 00:42:38,680
Maybe I bought it.

550
00:42:38,750 --> 00:42:41,480
And you're hoping to find a great secret in there?

551
00:42:41,550 --> 00:42:43,850
I've already found it.

552
00:42:43,920 --> 00:42:45,320
That's why I'm here.

553
00:42:51,530 --> 00:42:54,160
Tesla built one for another magician.

554
00:42:54,230 --> 00:42:56,000
Why would you want the same thing?

555
00:42:56,070 --> 00:42:58,060
Call it a professional rivalry.

556
00:42:58,140 --> 00:43:02,270
Mr. Tesla has built unusual machines for unusual people.

557
00:43:03,280 --> 00:43:06,370
But he would never talk about it. I understand discretion.

558
00:43:06,450 --> 00:43:07,740
I just want the machine.

559
00:43:11,080 --> 00:43:12,750
Finish your drink.

560
00:43:13,720 --> 00:43:16,420
I want to show you something. I think you'll have

561
00:43:16,490 --> 00:43:18,720
a special appreciation for our work.

562
00:43:18,790 --> 00:43:22,250
I thought it was a secret. You're a magician.

563
00:43:22,330 --> 00:43:24,060
Who's gonna believe you?

564
00:43:25,230 --> 00:43:27,430
Not long now.

565
00:43:27,500 --> 00:43:29,970
Our equipment requires a great deal of current.

566
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:31,770
Tesla electrified the whole town

567
00:43:31,840 --> 00:43:34,900
in exchange for using the generators when we need them.

568
00:43:36,110 --> 00:43:39,310
We do our tests when the townspeople are asleep.

569
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:42,080
Mr. Tesla doesn't want to scare anyone.

570
00:43:59,900 --> 00:44:01,920
Where are the wires?

571
00:44:03,300 --> 00:44:04,570
Exactly.

572
00:44:13,780 --> 00:44:16,480
Where's the generator?

573
00:44:16,550 --> 00:44:17,980
You saw it last week.

574
00:44:18,050 --> 00:44:21,150
That must be ten miles from here. Fifteen.

575
00:44:21,220 --> 00:44:25,320
And I have to ride all of them before I get to bed.

576
00:44:25,390 --> 00:44:28,090
I'll send word for you in a few days, Mr. Angier.

577
00:44:28,160 --> 00:44:32,260
Magic. Real magic.

578
00:44:32,330 --> 00:44:34,460
Now appearing, only here at Londors Royal Albert Hall,

579
00:44:36,500 --> 00:44:38,800
one of the miracles of our age.

580
00:44:38,870 --> 00:44:43,470
A technological marvel. You've never seen anything like it in your lives.

581
00:44:43,540 --> 00:44:46,270
Your eyes will not believe what they're witnessing.

582
00:44:46,350 --> 00:44:49,210
The miracle of Nikola Tesla, ladies and gentlemen.

583
00:44:49,280 --> 00:44:51,770
Free, clean power. Like to know the future?

584
00:44:51,850 --> 00:44:54,910
The man who's speaking right now is going to change the world.

585
00:44:54,990 --> 00:44:59,360
Come one, come all. Immediate seating, no waiting.

586
00:45:14,470 --> 00:45:15,700
Ladies and gentlemen,

587
00:45:17,810 --> 00:45:19,640
Objections have been raised...

588
00:45:19,710 --> 00:45:23,150
They should be raised. Does that look safe to you?

589
00:45:23,220 --> 00:45:25,780
Part of Thomas Edisors smear campaign

590
00:45:25,850 --> 00:45:31,550
against Mr. Tesla's superior alternating current.

591
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:34,460
We have asked Mr. Tesla to reconsider.

592
00:45:34,530 --> 00:45:37,020
Which he will not!

593
00:45:37,100 --> 00:45:42,260
But I've been told he refuses to appear under any such restrictions.

594
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:44,900
This thing's gonna blow.

595
00:45:46,240 --> 00:45:48,670
Ladies and gentlemen, please.

596
00:46:27,380 --> 00:46:28,510
Hey.

597
00:46:30,750 --> 00:46:33,180
Hello, Jess.

598
00:46:35,660 --> 00:46:39,460
Have you had a nice day with mommy? Huh?

599
00:46:40,390 --> 00:46:42,420
Sarah.

600
00:46:42,500 --> 00:46:45,400
I love you. See, today it's true.

601
00:46:45,470 --> 00:46:48,030
Yeah. Hello.

602
00:46:49,940 --> 00:46:52,300
I saw happiness.

603
00:46:52,370 --> 00:46:55,000
Happiness that should have been mine.

604
00:46:55,070 --> 00:46:57,100
But I was wrong.

605
00:46:57,180 --> 00:47:01,810
His notebook reveals that he never had the life that I envied.

606
00:47:01,880 --> 00:47:04,510
The family life that he craves one minute,

607
00:47:04,580 --> 00:47:08,880
he rails against the next, demanding freedom.

608
00:47:08,960 --> 00:47:11,950
His mind... his mind is a divided one.

609
00:47:12,030 --> 00:47:13,460
His soul is restless.

610
00:47:13,530 --> 00:47:15,650
His wife and child, tormented

611
00:47:15,730 --> 00:47:19,690
by his fickle and contradictory nature.

612
00:47:23,900 --> 00:47:26,390
So, what do you think you'll call her?

613
00:47:26,470 --> 00:47:27,840
I don't know.

614
00:47:27,910 --> 00:47:30,210
Everybody needs a name, so, what's hers?

615
00:47:31,110 --> 00:47:32,910
Maybe Sarah.

616
00:47:35,280 --> 00:47:37,680
That's a lovely name. That's a lovely name.

617
00:47:37,750 --> 00:47:40,980
Well, you talk about what you want to do for the rest of the day.

618
00:47:41,050 --> 00:47:42,920
All right, go on, you talk with her.

619
00:47:45,360 --> 00:47:49,620
Are they taking her away?

620
00:47:49,700 --> 00:47:51,600
To the workhouse?

621
00:47:54,430 --> 00:47:55,920
You keep talking.

622
00:47:58,640 --> 00:48:01,040
Tell Owens I've reconsidered. Take it.

623
00:48:01,110 --> 00:48:02,900
Go on, take it.

624
00:48:06,480 --> 00:48:08,610
It's for the best.

625
00:48:13,420 --> 00:48:16,010
Let me see.

626
00:48:16,090 --> 00:48:18,720
I'm gonna learn all the professor's secrets.

627
00:48:16,090 --> 00:48:18,720
教授：波顿的艺名

628
00:48:18,790 --> 00:48:22,230
Only if I teach you how to read.

629
00:48:22,300 --> 00:48:24,660
They're just stupid tricks, right?

630
00:48:24,730 --> 00:48:27,960
Haven't helped you get out of here, have they?

631
00:48:28,030 --> 00:48:31,370
Or can't you undo real locks, professor?

632
00:48:32,740 --> 00:48:35,400
Maybe I'm just biding my time.

633
00:48:35,480 --> 00:48:37,970
Maybe one day I'll...

634
00:48:38,040 --> 00:48:40,670
...open my hand, get your attention,

635
00:48:40,750 --> 00:48:43,650
ask, "Are you watching closely?"

636
00:48:43,720 --> 00:48:45,810
Maybe a magic whirl or two.

637
00:48:48,420 --> 00:48:50,250
And then I'll be gone.

638
00:48:53,860 --> 00:48:56,920
How'd you get so famous then?

639
00:48:57,830 --> 00:48:59,560
Magic.

640
00:49:02,030 --> 00:49:04,900
Oi! Borden, get back here!

641
00:49:04,970 --> 00:49:06,530
Thank you.

642
00:49:06,610 --> 00:49:08,570
Shut up!

643
00:49:11,880 --> 00:49:13,970
Where's the bloody key?!

644
00:49:20,590 --> 00:49:23,050
February 8, 1899.

645
00:49:23,120 --> 00:49:25,250
Today, finally, a breakthrough.

646
00:49:25,320 --> 00:49:28,050
Tesla's agreed to see me.

647
00:49:35,800 --> 00:49:36,960
Perfectly safe.

648
00:49:58,490 --> 00:50:02,260
So this is the Great Danton.

649
00:50:02,330 --> 00:50:06,920
Mr. Alley has effused about your act to me on any number of occasions.

650
00:50:08,770 --> 00:50:10,330
Hold out other hand.

651
00:50:18,910 --> 00:50:22,710
What's conducting the electricity? Our bodies, Mr. Angier.

652
00:50:22,780 --> 00:50:26,980
Quite capable of conducting and, indeed, producing energy.

653
00:50:28,520 --> 00:50:30,580
Have you eaten, Mr. Angier?

654
00:50:32,490 --> 00:50:35,650
I need something impossible.

655
00:50:37,430 --> 00:50:42,200
You're familiar with the phrase, "Mars reach exceeds his grasp"?

656
00:50:42,270 --> 00:50:43,960
Is a lie.

657
00:50:44,040 --> 00:50:46,060
Mars grasp exceeds his nerve.

658
00:50:47,270 --> 00:50:50,140
Society only tolerates one change at a time.

659
00:50:51,610 --> 00:50:54,880
First time I tried to change the world,

660
00:50:54,950 --> 00:50:56,710
I was hailed as a visionary.

661
00:50:57,220 --> 00:50:59,340
Second time...

662
00:50:59,420 --> 00:51:02,440
...I was asked politely to retire.

663
00:51:03,590 --> 00:51:07,120
So here I am, enjoying my retirement.

664
00:51:07,190 --> 00:51:11,060
Nothing is impossible, Mr. Angier. What you want is simply expensive.

665
00:51:11,130 --> 00:51:13,360
If I were to build for you this machine,

666
00:51:13,430 --> 00:51:16,490
you would be presenting it merely as illusion?

667
00:51:16,570 --> 00:51:19,900
Well, if people actually believed the things I did on stage,

668
00:51:19,970 --> 00:51:22,370
they wouldn't clap, they'd scream.

669
00:51:22,440 --> 00:51:24,430
Think of sawing a woman in half.

670
00:51:24,510 --> 00:51:26,840
Have you considered the cost of this?

671
00:51:26,910 --> 00:51:28,850
Price is not an object.

672
00:51:28,920 --> 00:51:32,870
Perhaps not, but have you considered the cost?

673
00:51:33,850 --> 00:51:35,180
I'm not sure I follow.

674
00:51:36,260 --> 00:51:38,090
Go home, forget this thing.

675
00:51:38,160 --> 00:51:40,350
I can recognize an obsession.

676
00:51:40,430 --> 00:51:44,450
No good will come of it. Hasn't good come of your obsessions?

677
00:51:44,530 --> 00:51:47,760
At first, but I have followed them too long.

678
00:51:47,830 --> 00:51:49,770
I am their slave.

679
00:51:49,840 --> 00:51:52,930
And one day, they will choose to destroy me.

680
00:51:54,740 --> 00:51:59,180
If you understand an obsession, then you know you won't change my mind.

681
00:52:00,110 --> 00:52:01,100
So be it.

682
00:52:02,620 --> 00:52:03,950
Will you build it?

683
00:52:04,920 --> 00:52:08,480
I've already begun to build it, Mr. Angier.

684
00:52:08,550 --> 00:52:12,550
I hope you enjoy the mountain air. This will take some time.

685
00:52:15,950 --> 00:52:18,950
教授起死回生 带来最新魔术“移形换影”

686
00:52:36,480 --> 00:52:39,180
Uh... I thought you'd gone.

687
00:52:40,320 --> 00:52:42,790
I don't really have anywhere to go.

688
00:52:44,760 --> 00:52:46,660
You've been sleeping here?

689
00:52:46,730 --> 00:52:51,460
Cutter said it'd be all right till we get another booking. What are you doing?

690
00:52:51,530 --> 00:52:53,760
Uh... research.

691
00:52:53,830 --> 00:52:55,270
Part of a magiciars job

692
00:52:55,330 --> 00:52:58,200
is to... watch his competition, see what illusions...

693
00:52:58,270 --> 00:53:01,210
You're going to do something to that man, aren't you?

694
00:53:01,270 --> 00:53:03,140
Cutter's hoping you let things lie.

695
00:53:03,210 --> 00:53:06,080
He says if Borden thinks things are even between you...

696
00:53:06,150 --> 00:53:07,980
Even?

697
00:53:08,050 --> 00:53:10,610
My wife for a couple of his fingers?

698
00:53:10,680 --> 00:53:13,780
He has a family now and he's performing again.

699
00:53:13,850 --> 00:53:16,690
Borden is out there living his life just as he intended,

700
00:53:16,760 --> 00:53:19,450
as if nothing had happened and look at me.

701
00:53:19,530 --> 00:53:22,020
I'm alone and no theater will touch me.

702
00:53:22,090 --> 00:53:23,690
Us.

703
00:53:23,760 --> 00:53:26,130
You're going to need a better disguise.

704
00:53:43,650 --> 00:53:45,840
I need a volunteer.

705
00:53:45,920 --> 00:53:47,390
What happened?

706
00:53:48,390 --> 00:53:50,220
Did you hurt him?

707
00:53:52,120 --> 00:53:53,420
What happened, Robert?

708
00:53:55,830 --> 00:53:59,790
You, sir. Just a rubber ball, yes?

709
00:53:59,870 --> 00:54:00,960
Thank you.

710
00:54:01,030 --> 00:54:03,630
Just a rubber ball? No.

711
00:54:03,700 --> 00:54:06,900
Not normal. Not a normal rubber ball.

712
00:54:06,970 --> 00:54:08,100
It's magic.

713
00:54:08,910 --> 00:54:10,970
He had a new trick.

714
00:54:24,990 --> 00:54:26,550
Was it good?

715
00:54:30,930 --> 00:54:34,890
It was the greatest magic trick I've ever seen.

716
00:54:38,800 --> 00:54:40,930
Did they applaud when you saw it?

717
00:54:41,010 --> 00:54:44,870
It was too good. It was too simple. The audience hardly had time to see it.

718
00:54:44,940 --> 00:54:47,810
He's a dreadful magician. A wonderful magician.

719
00:54:47,880 --> 00:54:50,410
He's a dreadful showman. He doesn't dress it up,

720
00:54:50,480 --> 00:54:52,310
how to sell it. How does he do it?

721
00:54:52,380 --> 00:54:54,410
He uses a double. No, it's too simple.

722
00:54:54,490 --> 00:54:55,980
This is a complex illusion.

723
00:54:56,060 --> 00:54:58,460
You say that because you don't know the method.

724
00:54:58,520 --> 00:55:00,920
It's a double that comes out. It's the only way.

725
00:55:00,990 --> 00:55:04,900
I've seen him perform it three times. The prestige is the same man.

726
00:55:04,960 --> 00:55:08,490
No, it's not. The same man comes out of the cabinet.

727
00:55:08,570 --> 00:55:10,830
It's the same man.

728
00:55:10,900 --> 00:55:13,600
He wears padded gloves to hide his damaged fingers,

729
00:55:13,670 --> 00:55:16,270
but, if you look closely, you can tell.

730
00:55:17,980 --> 00:55:20,840
He doesn't know how to sell it to an audience, but I do.

731
00:55:20,910 --> 00:55:23,350
Yeah, we could use it as a climax to the show.

732
00:55:23,420 --> 00:55:25,820
Yes.

733
00:55:25,890 --> 00:55:29,080
The man stole my life, I'm gonna steal his trick.

734
00:55:29,160 --> 00:55:31,320
We got to find someone who looks like you.

735
00:55:31,390 --> 00:55:34,920
He doesn't use a double. I don't know how Borden does it.

736
00:55:34,990 --> 00:55:39,490
Either... You either wait for him to retire and buy the secret,

737
00:55:39,570 --> 00:55:41,360
or you listen to how I would do it.

738
00:55:41,430 --> 00:55:43,960
And the only way that I know how to do it

739
00:55:44,040 --> 00:55:46,500
is to find you a bloody good double.

740
00:55:47,510 --> 00:55:48,910
All right.

741
00:55:50,340 --> 00:55:52,830
Take a good look. Let's get out there and find me.

742
00:55:55,110 --> 00:55:56,600
Look, look.

743
00:56:01,150 --> 00:56:02,680
What's that?

744
00:56:06,030 --> 00:56:08,320
It's for you. What's this for?

745
00:56:08,960 --> 00:56:10,790
Come.

746
00:56:13,930 --> 00:56:15,700
You...

747
00:56:15,770 --> 00:56:19,800
I asked you last week... You caught me in a wrong mood.

748
00:56:19,870 --> 00:56:22,070
But you won't ever... Sarah,

749
00:56:22,140 --> 00:56:25,300
I'm gonna have to change my mind, aren't I?

750
00:56:25,380 --> 00:56:30,040
The act is taking off. Soon we'll be in a bigger theater, things will work out.

751
00:56:32,620 --> 00:56:36,420
I can't believe it. Thank you. Thank you. It's beautiful.

752
00:56:43,060 --> 00:56:46,160
Gerry? Darling, look at you.

753
00:56:46,230 --> 00:56:49,200
Mr. Cutter, Mr. Angier, I'd like you to meet Gerald Root.

754
00:56:54,270 --> 00:56:58,210
A pleasure. Pleasure to meet you fine gentlemen.

755
00:56:58,280 --> 00:57:02,010
Would you like for me to tell you a little joke? Come here.

756
00:57:05,050 --> 00:57:08,140
Yes. Are you laughing now?

757
00:57:11,590 --> 00:57:13,220
I have to take a piss.

758
00:57:14,630 --> 00:57:17,720
He's out of his mind. Of course, he's an out-of-work actor.

759
00:57:17,800 --> 00:57:19,700
He's perfect. He needs a little help.

760
00:57:19,770 --> 00:57:22,730
But when I get done with him, he could be your brother.

761
00:57:22,800 --> 00:57:25,400
I don't need him to be my brother, I need him to be me.

762
00:57:25,470 --> 00:57:27,170
Give me a month.

763
00:57:33,950 --> 00:57:36,570
So you open the door...

764
00:57:36,650 --> 00:57:38,120
...and...

765
00:57:45,490 --> 00:57:47,250
Come on.

766
00:57:50,960 --> 00:57:52,900
Couldn't you have found a softer one?

767
00:57:52,970 --> 00:57:55,300
Well, it's not for sleeping on.

768
00:57:55,370 --> 00:57:57,430
So, if you come down through there...

769
00:58:00,110 --> 00:58:03,130
...does Root go up through there? Yep.

770
00:58:03,210 --> 00:58:05,270
It's going to be amazing.

771
00:58:05,340 --> 00:58:08,800
Well, it has to be. Borders trick is getting noticed.

772
00:58:11,650 --> 00:58:14,020
The place was packed today.

773
00:58:15,620 --> 00:58:17,420
You went and saw his show again.

774
00:58:17,490 --> 00:58:19,920
Ready to meet yourself, Mr. Angier?

775
00:58:28,300 --> 00:58:30,630
All I have to do is keep myself stinking drunk,

776
00:58:30,700 --> 00:58:33,040
and no one will be able to tell the difference.

777
00:58:33,110 --> 00:58:35,200
Have a little faith, sir.

778
00:58:35,270 --> 00:58:39,180
Now, would you favor us with a performance, Mr. Root?

779
00:58:59,930 --> 00:59:04,100
You'd drink, too, if you knew the world half as well as I do.

780
00:59:07,810 --> 00:59:09,500
Did you think you were unique?

781
00:59:09,580 --> 00:59:12,270
I've been Caesar, I've played Faust.

782
00:59:12,340 --> 00:59:15,440
How difficult could it possibly be to play the Great Danton?

783
00:59:15,510 --> 00:59:18,640
You can go back to being yourself now, Root, for nothing.

784
00:59:18,720 --> 00:59:20,980
I'd rather be him for now.

785
00:59:21,050 --> 00:59:23,820
I find it, uh... amusing.

786
00:59:27,260 --> 00:59:28,990
"My liege,

787
00:59:27,260 --> 00:59:32,390
《亨利四世》台词

788
00:59:29,060 --> 00:59:32,390
I did deny no prisoners."

789
00:59:32,460 --> 00:59:34,630
You look wonderful. Oh, thank you.

790
00:59:34,700 --> 00:59:37,970
Root has to keep a low profile. Anyone sees him, the game's up.

791
00:59:38,040 --> 00:59:40,830
I don't know how you do things. I'm not sure I wanna know.

792
00:59:40,910 --> 00:59:43,240
Have you thought what we should call it?

793
00:59:43,310 --> 00:59:47,470
There's no point being coy. Borden calls his trick The Transported Man.

794
00:59:51,380 --> 00:59:54,550
Ladies and gentlemen, much of what you've seen may be termed

795
00:59:54,620 --> 00:59:58,390
as illusions or entertaining trifles.

796
01:00:03,060 --> 01:00:04,120
Alas...

797
01:00:06,300 --> 01:00:08,820
...I cannot claim this next feat as illusion.

798
01:00:09,740 --> 01:00:10,930
Watch carefully.

799
01:00:12,370 --> 01:00:14,810
You will see no trickery,

800
01:00:14,870 --> 01:00:17,970
for no trickery is employed.

801
01:00:18,040 --> 01:00:21,410
Merely a technique familiar to certain citizens of the Orient

802
01:00:21,480 --> 01:00:23,640
and various holy men of the Himalayas.

803
01:00:26,920 --> 01:00:29,950
Indeed, many of you may be familiar with this technique,

804
01:00:30,020 --> 01:00:33,190
but for those of you who are not, do not be alarmed.

805
01:00:33,260 --> 01:00:36,320
What you're about to see is considered safe.

806
01:01:07,890 --> 01:01:10,950
To our achievement.

807
01:01:11,030 --> 01:01:13,790
The manager says he's never seen a reaction like it.

808
01:01:13,870 --> 01:01:17,170
At least he saw it. I spent the ovation hiding under the stage.

809
01:01:17,240 --> 01:01:19,570
No one cares about the man who disappears.

810
01:01:19,640 --> 01:01:21,630
They care about the one who comes out.

811
01:01:21,710 --> 01:01:23,770
I care about the man in the box.

812
01:01:23,840 --> 01:01:28,140
Thank you. I don't know, maybe we could switch before the trick.

813
01:01:28,210 --> 01:01:30,370
I'm the prestige, and Root's below stage.

814
01:01:30,450 --> 01:01:33,380
No, the anticipation of the trick is everything.

815
01:01:33,450 --> 01:01:36,510
We need your showmanship to build up the suspense.

816
01:01:36,590 --> 01:01:40,150
Once Root opens his mouth, it's over. He can't introduce this trick.

817
01:01:40,230 --> 01:01:42,960
Of course I can. I'm the Great Danton.

818
01:01:43,030 --> 01:01:46,620
Root, you bloody fool, get out of that wardrobe and makeup.

819
01:01:46,700 --> 01:01:49,360
Anyone could walk in here at any minute.

820
01:01:49,430 --> 01:01:51,490
Congratulations, all.

821
01:01:51,570 --> 01:01:55,270
Life is not full of these moments. We worked hard.

822
01:01:55,340 --> 01:01:57,000
We need to celebrate properly.

823
01:02:00,750 --> 01:02:03,210
What's wrong? Is it your wife?

824
01:02:03,280 --> 01:02:05,580
No, it's the trick. It isn't good enough.

825
01:02:05,650 --> 01:02:08,980
Borders trick is nothing compared to ours. He has no style.

826
01:02:09,050 --> 01:02:12,920
He doesn't spend the finale hiding under the stage.

827
01:02:12,990 --> 01:02:15,890
I need to know how he does it. Why?

828
01:02:15,960 --> 01:02:18,450
So I can do it better.

829
01:02:20,530 --> 01:02:23,000
I need you to go and work for him.

830
01:02:26,040 --> 01:02:27,770
Work for him? Are you joking?

831
01:02:29,040 --> 01:02:30,240
You'll be my spy.

832
01:02:30,310 --> 01:02:32,500
We just got our start. You want me to leave?

833
01:02:32,580 --> 01:02:34,670
That's how we advance. Think of it.

834
01:02:34,750 --> 01:02:37,940
People are excited by Cutter's version. Imagine what we'd do

835
01:02:38,020 --> 01:02:42,450
with the real illusion. We'd have the greatest magic act anyone's ever seen.

836
01:02:42,520 --> 01:02:46,290
He knows I work for you. Exactly why he'll want to hire you.

837
01:02:46,360 --> 01:02:48,920
He'll want my secret. Why would he trust me?

838
01:02:48,990 --> 01:02:51,690
Because you're gonna tell him the truth.

839
01:02:54,600 --> 01:02:56,430
Hmm?

840
01:03:03,980 --> 01:03:05,100
Good girl.

841
01:03:08,280 --> 01:03:11,810
You must be curious to see what so much money has bought you.

842
01:03:12,480 --> 01:03:15,390
Fitting you should be here for her maiden voyage.

843
01:03:15,450 --> 01:03:17,290
Your hat.

844
01:03:25,230 --> 01:03:27,820
You might want to stand back.

845
01:03:48,290 --> 01:03:49,410
I don't understand.

846
01:03:49,490 --> 01:03:51,820
Perhaps it would be best if you left us to it.

847
01:03:51,890 --> 01:03:53,520
Is there a problem, Mr. Tesla?

848
01:03:53,590 --> 01:03:55,620
No. Come back next week. Next week?

849
01:03:55,690 --> 01:03:58,250
Next week, it will be fine. Tesla!

850
01:03:58,330 --> 01:04:00,060
Just a little temperamental.

851
01:04:05,900 --> 01:04:08,810
Interesting workshop. Yeah, we make do.

852
01:04:08,870 --> 01:04:11,040
My name is Olivia... I know who you are.

853
01:04:11,110 --> 01:04:12,770
Here to steal the rest of my show?

854
01:04:12,840 --> 01:04:14,740
To give your show what it's missing.

855
01:04:14,810 --> 01:04:18,040
Yeah, what might that be? Me.

856
01:04:18,120 --> 01:04:22,580
I was just saying that, wasn't I?

857
01:04:22,650 --> 01:04:24,950
A womars touch. I've left Angier.

858
01:04:25,020 --> 01:04:27,080
All right. I want a job.

859
01:04:27,160 --> 01:04:28,490
Yeah.

860
01:04:28,560 --> 01:04:30,390
I know you've no reason to trust me.

861
01:04:30,460 --> 01:04:32,020
Why wouldn't I trust you?

862
01:04:32,100 --> 01:04:35,530
You're only the mistress of my enemy. Why wouldn't I trust you?

863
01:04:37,200 --> 01:04:39,140
Mr. Borden. Alfred.

864
01:04:41,570 --> 01:04:43,230
I'm going to tell you the truth.

865
01:04:43,310 --> 01:04:47,940
Now that is a slippery notion in our line of work, isn't it, Miss Wenscombe?

866
01:04:50,050 --> 01:04:52,710
I am here because he sent me here.

867
01:04:52,780 --> 01:04:55,250
He wants me to work for you and steal your secret.

868
01:04:55,320 --> 01:04:58,690
What does he need my secret for? His trick is topnotch.

869
01:04:58,760 --> 01:05:01,350
He vanishes, and then he reappears instantly

870
01:05:01,430 --> 01:05:03,920
on the other side of the stage, mute, overweight

871
01:05:04,000 --> 01:05:06,560
and, unless I'm mistaken, very drunk.

872
01:05:06,630 --> 01:05:10,120
It's astonishing. How does he do it? And tell me, Olivia,

873
01:05:10,200 --> 01:05:13,660
does he enjoy taking his bows under the stage?

874
01:05:13,740 --> 01:05:17,040
It's killing him. He's obsessed with discovering your methods.

875
01:05:17,110 --> 01:05:20,170
He thinks of nothing else, takes no pleasure in our success.

876
01:05:20,250 --> 01:05:21,740
And I've had enough.

877
01:05:21,810 --> 01:05:24,210
There is no future with him.

878
01:05:25,350 --> 01:05:29,080
He sent me here to steal your secrets, but I've come to offer you his.

879
01:05:29,150 --> 01:05:33,060
This is the truth... is it?

880
01:05:37,600 --> 01:05:39,890
Think you'd better get dressed, sir.

881
01:05:39,970 --> 01:05:43,990
Root, you're late and more drunk than usual. Get down below stairs right away.

882
01:05:44,070 --> 01:05:45,800
No.

883
01:05:45,870 --> 01:05:49,240
No, we need to have a little chat, Mr. Cutter.

884
01:05:49,310 --> 01:05:53,640
We have a problem. Cutter, Borden is performing right across the street.

885
01:05:53,710 --> 01:05:56,980
Yeah, we have a bigger problem.

886
01:05:57,050 --> 01:06:00,040
Root. He's realized that he can make demands.

887
01:06:00,120 --> 01:06:02,210
Wha? He's blackmailing us?

888
01:06:02,290 --> 01:06:04,260
I was surprised, to tell you the truth.

889
01:06:04,320 --> 01:06:06,920
It usually takes them a lot longer to figure it out.

890
01:06:06,990 --> 01:06:09,550
How much does he want? It makes no difference.

891
01:06:09,630 --> 01:06:13,090
We've got to stop doing the trick. Stop the trick? Look at this.

892
01:06:13,170 --> 01:06:19,000
Last week they said you were the premier stage performer in London.

893
01:06:19,070 --> 01:06:22,440
Not magician, mind you. Performer of any kind.

894
01:06:22,510 --> 01:06:25,740
What's your point? My point is, you've climbed too high

895
01:06:25,810 --> 01:06:28,250
to get away with professional embarrassment.

896
01:06:28,310 --> 01:06:31,680
We don't do any tricks we can't control.

897
01:06:33,850 --> 01:06:37,150
Just pay him for now. We keep doing the trick until Borden opens,

898
01:06:37,220 --> 01:06:39,190
and then we'll phase it out.

899
01:06:40,090 --> 01:06:41,580
All right.

900
01:06:44,360 --> 01:06:46,490
Cutter was always surprised

901
01:06:46,570 --> 01:06:48,470
how fast Root turned bad.

902
01:06:48,530 --> 01:06:53,100
To what do I owe the pleasure of this rather welcome pint of ale?

903
01:06:53,170 --> 01:06:56,300
You're the Great Danton, aren't you? Sh, sh, sh, sh, sh.

904
01:06:56,370 --> 01:07:00,040
Of course I am, but don't advertise it, because I'll be mobbed with fans.

905
01:07:00,110 --> 01:07:02,550
We paid him enough to keep him in beers.

906
01:07:02,610 --> 01:07:04,710
You wouldn't expect him to rock the boat.

907
01:07:04,780 --> 01:07:07,380
Many of you may be familiar with this technique,

908
01:07:07,450 --> 01:07:10,940
but for those of you who aren't, do not be alarmed.

909
01:07:11,020 --> 01:07:14,190
What you're about to see is considered safe.

910
01:07:14,260 --> 01:07:17,890
And who are you? I am a humble admirer

911
01:07:17,960 --> 01:07:20,560
and a fellow practitioner.

912
01:07:20,630 --> 01:07:23,000
Mmm. Very good.

913
01:07:32,380 --> 01:07:35,210
Another one? Oh, if you insist.

914
01:07:35,280 --> 01:07:38,010
Another. I'm not performing tonight.

915
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:40,110
I'm only doing one show, but to be frank,

916
01:07:40,190 --> 01:07:42,520
my people pretty much run things these days.

917
01:07:42,590 --> 01:07:43,780
Get up there.

918
01:07:45,160 --> 01:07:46,210
Get up!

919
01:07:46,290 --> 01:07:49,060
Your illusion, The Transported Man,

920
01:07:49,130 --> 01:07:51,720
I'm not claiming to know your methods,

921
01:07:51,800 --> 01:07:55,990
but I had a similar trick in my act,

922
01:07:56,070 --> 01:07:58,900
and, uh... I used a double.

923
01:07:58,970 --> 01:08:04,170
Mm, I see, very good. Well, it was, and then it went bad.

924
01:08:04,240 --> 01:08:07,640
What I didn't count on was that when I incorporated this bloke

925
01:08:07,710 --> 01:08:09,810
into my act,

926
01:08:09,880 --> 01:08:11,870
he had complete power over me.

927
01:08:13,020 --> 01:08:14,650
Complete power, you say?

928
01:08:32,140 --> 01:08:37,400
Be very careful giving someone that power over you.

929
01:08:37,480 --> 01:08:41,810
Huh. Thank you. Um...

930
01:08:41,880 --> 01:08:44,750
Yes, thank you for the warning.

931
01:08:44,820 --> 01:08:47,510
Hmm. Cheers.

932
01:08:48,950 --> 01:08:51,080
I cannot claim this next feat as illusion.

933
01:08:51,160 --> 01:08:54,520
What you're about to see is considered safe.

934
01:09:32,760 --> 01:09:37,720
Oh! The Great Danton.

935
01:09:39,130 --> 01:09:41,380
教授在潘太及斯剧院德首演

936
01:09:43,940 --> 01:09:45,810
I apologize.

937
01:09:45,880 --> 01:09:49,640
There simply is too much magic...

938
01:09:49,710 --> 01:09:54,710
...for my stage at The Pantages across the street.

939
01:09:58,760 --> 01:10:01,690
Pardon my intrusion.

940
01:10:03,760 --> 01:10:08,170
And go easy on the poor chap. He does try so very hard.

941
01:10:11,040 --> 01:10:13,470
I don't know how Borden found him.

942
01:10:13,540 --> 01:10:15,440
Kept him under wraps. I was careful.

943
01:10:15,510 --> 01:10:17,170
Yeah, well, he did.

944
01:10:18,340 --> 01:10:20,330
Do you think it was her?

945
01:10:26,580 --> 01:10:29,550
You werert expecting me?

946
01:10:29,620 --> 01:10:32,350
I expected you sooner. Your message said afternoon.

947
01:10:32,420 --> 01:10:34,690
It takes a bit for me to get around these days.

948
01:10:34,760 --> 01:10:38,820
He's taken everything from me. My wife, my career,

949
01:10:38,900 --> 01:10:40,760
now you. You sent me.

950
01:10:40,830 --> 01:10:43,890
I sent you to steal his secret, not to improve his act.

951
01:10:43,970 --> 01:10:45,530
It's my job. Or fall in love!

952
01:10:45,600 --> 01:10:48,370
I did everything you asked! Yes? Yes?

953
01:10:48,440 --> 01:10:50,930
How does he do it? Cutter was right, a double.

954
01:10:51,010 --> 01:10:53,240
'Course he said that. He didn't say it.

955
01:10:53,310 --> 01:10:56,750
I've seen things, makeup, glasses, wigs. We don't use for the show.

956
01:10:56,820 --> 01:10:59,550
I've seen it hidden backstage. Misdirection.

957
01:10:59,620 --> 01:11:02,310
He leaves them lying around to make you think that.

958
01:11:02,390 --> 01:11:04,250
He doesn't know when I'm looking.

959
01:11:04,320 --> 01:11:07,550
All the time, Olivia! That's who he is!

960
01:11:07,630 --> 01:11:08,890
That's what it takes!

961
01:11:08,960 --> 01:11:11,390
He lives his act, don't you see?

962
01:11:13,430 --> 01:11:16,800
Just because you're sleeping with him doesn't mean he trusts you.

963
01:11:16,870 --> 01:11:19,270
You think you can see everything, don't you?

964
01:11:19,340 --> 01:11:22,170
The Great Danton is a blind fool.

965
01:11:22,240 --> 01:11:23,640
His notebook.

966
01:11:25,010 --> 01:11:27,270
You stole it?

967
01:11:27,350 --> 01:11:29,140
I borrowed it for tonight.

968
01:11:29,210 --> 01:11:31,410
I thought you could translate some of it...

969
01:11:31,480 --> 01:11:35,540
I can't. No one can. It's a cipher.

970
01:11:35,620 --> 01:11:39,060
Even with the keyword, it will take months to decode.

971
01:11:39,120 --> 01:11:42,030
And without the keyword? Perhaps never.

972
01:11:42,090 --> 01:11:43,720
We'll see. We will not see.

973
01:11:43,800 --> 01:11:46,730
If I don't get that back by tomorrow he'll know I took it.

974
01:11:46,800 --> 01:11:49,100
Leave him. I can't. He knows where I live.

975
01:11:50,440 --> 01:11:55,240
This is his diary, Olivia. All of his secrets are right here in my hands.

976
01:11:55,910 --> 01:11:57,500
It won't bring your wife back.

977
01:11:57,580 --> 01:12:00,270
I don't care about my wife. I care about his secret.

978
01:12:13,530 --> 01:12:16,260
Look, I'll, um...

979
01:12:16,330 --> 01:12:18,660
I'll go to his workshop and stage a break-in.

980
01:12:18,730 --> 01:12:21,930
He'll know you took it. Yes, me. Not you.

981
01:12:23,030 --> 01:12:25,030
Understand?

982
01:12:33,480 --> 01:12:34,470
Robert.

983
01:12:36,880 --> 01:12:39,680
I have fallen in love with him.

984
01:12:40,590 --> 01:12:43,050
Then I know how hard this has been for you.

985
01:12:51,600 --> 01:12:53,090
Notebook?

986
01:12:55,730 --> 01:12:57,720
Then he's just getting started.

987
01:12:58,310 --> 01:13:01,360
潘太及斯剧院

988
01:13:26,970 --> 01:13:28,430
Professor!

989
01:13:28,500 --> 01:13:29,990
Good evening.

990
01:13:34,870 --> 01:13:36,500
I'm walking tonight.

991
01:13:36,570 --> 01:13:39,130
Let him come. I don't care.

992
01:13:39,210 --> 01:13:41,910
Professor! Professor!

993
01:15:05,800 --> 01:15:08,360
You all right? I'm alive.

994
01:15:08,430 --> 01:15:10,530
Saves me cutting you an air hole.

995
01:15:24,620 --> 01:15:27,180
I'm impressed. Why is that?

996
01:15:27,250 --> 01:15:30,220
You're finally getting your hands dirty.

997
01:15:30,290 --> 01:15:33,190
It's what a good trick costs, Angier.

998
01:15:33,260 --> 01:15:36,060
Risk, sacrifice.

999
01:15:36,130 --> 01:15:38,620
The sacrifice, I'm afraid, is all gonna be yours,

1000
01:15:38,700 --> 01:15:40,320
unless you give me what I want.

1001
01:15:41,700 --> 01:15:43,830
Which is? Your secret.

1002
01:15:43,900 --> 01:15:45,300
My secret?

1003
01:15:45,370 --> 01:15:48,500
Your method for The Transported Man.

1004
01:15:48,570 --> 01:15:51,410
Fallon wouldn't tell me. He doesn't talk at all.

1005
01:15:51,480 --> 01:15:54,970
You have my notebook. Useless without the keyword.

1006
01:15:56,450 --> 01:16:00,010
Write down your method, Mr. Borden, describe it in full.

1007
01:16:13,130 --> 01:16:15,500
I want the whole method, not the keyword.

1008
01:16:15,570 --> 01:16:17,970
I don't know if your secret's in your notebook.

1009
01:16:18,040 --> 01:16:20,500
The keyword is the method.

1010
01:16:24,510 --> 01:16:25,810
Where's my ingenieur?

1011
01:16:32,950 --> 01:16:35,580
Alive? How fast can you dig?

1012
01:16:38,060 --> 01:16:41,080
Fallon, you hear me? Fallon!

1013
01:16:42,390 --> 01:16:45,060
How's the arm? Still attached.

1014
01:16:46,500 --> 01:16:48,060
Did you find your answer?

1015
01:16:48,130 --> 01:16:51,900
Our answer, Cutter. I haven't looked yet. I wanted you to share this.

1016
01:16:51,970 --> 01:16:54,400
I already know how he does it, Robert.

1017
01:16:54,470 --> 01:16:57,570
Same way he always has, the same way as we do.

1018
01:16:57,640 --> 01:17:00,110
It's just that you want something more.

1019
01:17:00,180 --> 01:17:02,940
Well, let's find out then, shall we?

1020
01:17:07,250 --> 01:17:08,680
What does it mean?

1021
01:17:16,530 --> 01:17:19,000
It means, Cutter, we have a journey ahead of us.

1022
01:17:19,060 --> 01:17:21,930
To America. Robert?

1023
01:17:22,000 --> 01:17:24,970
Listen to me.

1024
01:17:25,040 --> 01:17:28,490
Obsession is a young mars game.

1025
01:17:28,570 --> 01:17:30,100
Come on.

1026
01:17:30,170 --> 01:17:32,640
I can't follow you any further in it.

1027
01:17:33,710 --> 01:17:35,650
I can't, I'm sorry.

1028
01:17:39,750 --> 01:17:41,450
Then the rest is up to me.

1029
01:17:43,520 --> 01:17:45,050
I'm sorry.

1030
01:17:50,360 --> 01:17:53,960
Good evening. Hello, darling.

1031
01:17:54,030 --> 01:17:55,520
Champagne, your finest.

1032
01:17:55,600 --> 01:17:58,300
I didn't know we were going to be joined for dinner.

1033
01:17:58,370 --> 01:18:00,670
Absolutely, we are celebrating.

1034
01:18:00,740 --> 01:18:02,430
Miss Wenscombe, Mr. Fallon.

1035
01:18:03,440 --> 01:18:06,310
What are we celebrating? We've hit upon a new trick.

1036
01:18:06,380 --> 01:18:08,440
Haven't we? What trick, Freddie?

1037
01:18:08,510 --> 01:18:10,540
Yes, Freddie, what trick?

1038
01:18:10,620 --> 01:18:15,020
I am going to bury myself alive every night,

1039
01:18:15,090 --> 01:18:18,950
and then someone will come along and dig me up. Wonderful.

1040
01:18:19,020 --> 01:18:22,120
I think he's had quite enough... No, pour the champagne.

1041
01:18:22,190 --> 01:18:25,890
Pour the... Sarah, don't talk to me like that. I'm not a bloody child.

1042
01:18:25,960 --> 01:18:29,160
Perhaps it would be better... Mr. Fallon, escort her home.

1043
01:18:29,230 --> 01:18:32,070
My husband's being a bore. Oh, please.

1044
01:18:32,140 --> 01:18:35,940
I see no reason you should suffer. Don't ruin this evening.

1045
01:18:38,340 --> 01:18:41,240
Good night, Mrs. Borden. Good night, Freddie.

1046
01:18:45,680 --> 01:18:47,980
Freddie? That's my name.

1047
01:18:48,050 --> 01:18:51,420
Not at home. I'm not always at home, am I?

1048
01:18:53,220 --> 01:18:55,890
Couldn't you have at least taken off the beard?

1049
01:18:55,960 --> 01:19:00,120
Sarah, I just came from the bloody theater just now.

1050
01:19:00,200 --> 01:19:03,260
All right? I'm out in public. Everybody else loves it.

1051
01:19:03,330 --> 01:19:05,270
Why are you being like this?

1052
01:19:05,340 --> 01:19:08,240
I had a terrible ordeal today.

1053
01:19:08,310 --> 01:19:12,070
I thought that something very precious...

1054
01:19:13,240 --> 01:19:15,180
...had been lost to me.

1055
01:19:15,250 --> 01:19:18,370
So I just wanted to celebrate just a little.

1056
01:19:18,450 --> 01:19:21,910
All right, what? What did you lose?

1057
01:19:27,060 --> 01:19:28,620
I see, more secrets.

1058
01:19:29,560 --> 01:19:32,720
Sarah, secrets are my life.

1059
01:19:34,670 --> 01:19:37,930
Our life. No, Alfred, stop, this isn't you.

1060
01:19:38,000 --> 01:19:40,470
Stop performing.

1061
01:20:04,630 --> 01:20:07,120
I thought I had the place to myself.

1062
01:20:07,200 --> 01:20:10,500
Unexpected guests, not very polite.

1063
01:20:10,570 --> 01:20:12,400
A lot of questions.

1064
01:20:12,470 --> 01:20:15,270
At first, I thought they worked for the government.

1065
01:20:15,340 --> 01:20:19,000
No? Worse, they work for Thomas Edison.

1066
01:20:20,980 --> 01:20:23,470
Today, a most curious development.

1067
01:20:23,550 --> 01:20:26,610
His assistant came to us with a proposition.

1068
01:20:28,250 --> 01:20:31,650
Obviously, Angier has sent her and told her to admit as much.

1069
01:20:31,720 --> 01:20:34,520
Does he enjoy taking his bows under the stage?

1070
01:20:34,590 --> 01:20:38,360
He sent me here to steal your secrets, but I've come to offer you his.

1071
01:20:38,430 --> 01:20:41,400
This is the truth...

1072
01:20:41,470 --> 01:20:42,450
...is it?

1073
01:20:50,510 --> 01:20:53,970
No, that's what he told me to tell you. The truth...

1074
01:20:54,050 --> 01:20:57,880
...is that I loved him and I stood by him and he sent me to you,

1075
01:20:57,950 --> 01:21:01,480
like he would send a stagehand to pick up his shirts.

1076
01:21:01,550 --> 01:21:04,350
I hate him for that.

1077
01:21:04,420 --> 01:21:07,650
I could spot Angier's methods from the back of the theater.

1078
01:21:09,360 --> 01:21:11,620
So, what could you possibly have to offer me?

1079
01:21:11,700 --> 01:21:13,320
You may know his tricks,

1080
01:21:13,400 --> 01:21:16,460
but you can't understand why no one sees yours are better.

1081
01:21:16,530 --> 01:21:19,090
You hide this. I had to look very closely

1082
01:21:19,170 --> 01:21:21,500
to spot it during The Transported Man,

1083
01:21:21,570 --> 01:21:24,410
but this makes you unique.

1084
01:21:24,480 --> 01:21:26,500
It shows you aren't using a double.

1085
01:21:26,580 --> 01:21:29,070
You mustrt hide it. You must display it proudly.

1086
01:21:29,150 --> 01:21:33,910
I'm sure it takes great skill to perform illusions with one good hand.

1087
01:21:33,980 --> 01:21:35,750
Yeah, it does.

1088
01:21:37,520 --> 01:21:40,680
So let people know.

1089
01:21:40,760 --> 01:21:44,250
You can be so much more than he is and I can show you how.

1090
01:21:45,960 --> 01:21:48,560
I think she's telling the truth.

1091
01:21:48,630 --> 01:21:50,760
I think we cannot trust her.

1092
01:21:54,310 --> 01:21:56,770
But I love her. I need her.

1093
01:21:57,980 --> 01:22:01,310
To open myself to such a relationship,

1094
01:22:01,380 --> 01:22:05,210
to the dangers of such an affair,

1095
01:22:05,280 --> 01:22:10,080
I need assurances of fidelity, of love.

1096
01:22:11,120 --> 01:22:12,710
But how to be sure?

1097
01:22:14,930 --> 01:22:19,260
I know a way. It's the only way to know her mind.

1098
01:22:19,330 --> 01:22:21,160
How could he send you away?

1099
01:22:23,030 --> 01:22:26,130
She must help me rid ourselves of Angier.

1100
01:22:32,740 --> 01:22:35,740
Today, my mistress proves her truthfulness.

1101
01:22:35,810 --> 01:22:37,540
Not to me, you understand.

1102
01:22:37,620 --> 01:22:40,240
I've been convinced since she led me to Root.

1103
01:22:40,320 --> 01:22:43,840
Today, Olivia proves her love for me

1104
01:22:43,920 --> 01:22:46,390
to you, Angier.

1105
01:22:46,460 --> 01:22:50,860
Yes, Angier, she gave you this notebook at my request.

1106
01:22:50,930 --> 01:22:54,360
And yes, "Tesla" is merely the key to my dairy,

1107
01:22:54,430 --> 01:22:57,230
not to my trick. You really think I'd part

1108
01:22:57,300 --> 01:23:00,430
with my secret so easily after so much?

1109
01:23:00,500 --> 01:23:02,170
Goodbye, Angier.

1110
01:23:02,240 --> 01:23:04,610
May you find solace for your forward ambition

1111
01:23:04,680 --> 01:23:06,470
back in your American home.

1112
01:23:15,990 --> 01:23:17,610
Tesla!

1113
01:23:19,560 --> 01:23:21,960
Tesla! Alley!

1114
01:23:23,160 --> 01:23:24,150
Alley!

1115
01:23:24,230 --> 01:23:26,820
Tesla never made a machine like the one I asked for.

1116
01:23:26,900 --> 01:23:29,700
I never said he had. You let me believe that he had.

1117
01:23:29,770 --> 01:23:32,460
You stole my money because your funding was cut off.

1118
01:23:32,540 --> 01:23:35,440
You've been shooting sparks in my top hat,

1119
01:23:35,510 --> 01:23:39,000
laughing at me all along, while using my money to stave off ruin.

1120
01:23:39,840 --> 01:23:41,970
I have seen Edisors men. Where?

1121
01:23:42,050 --> 01:23:44,540
In the hotel, I have a mind to bring them up here.

1122
01:23:44,620 --> 01:23:47,310
That would be unwise, Mr. Angier.

1123
01:23:47,380 --> 01:23:50,510
It is true that you are our last remaining financier,

1124
01:23:50,590 --> 01:23:52,450
but we have not stolen your money.

1125
01:23:52,520 --> 01:23:53,990
This is my cat.

1126
01:23:54,060 --> 01:23:57,120
When I told you I could make you a machine, I spoke a truth.

1127
01:23:57,190 --> 01:23:58,290
Why isn't it working?

1128
01:23:58,360 --> 01:24:00,690
Because exact science, Mr. Angier,

1129
01:24:00,760 --> 01:24:02,160
is not an exact science.

1130
01:24:02,230 --> 01:24:05,400
The machine simply does not operate as expected.

1131
01:24:05,470 --> 01:24:09,500
It requires further examination. So where did my top hat go?

1132
01:24:09,570 --> 01:24:10,630
Nowhere.

1133
01:24:10,710 --> 01:24:13,510
We tried the damn thing a dozen times.

1134
01:24:13,580 --> 01:24:15,200
The hat went nowhere.

1135
01:24:15,280 --> 01:24:17,180
We need to try different material.

1136
01:24:17,250 --> 01:24:19,410
It may provoke a different result.

1137
01:24:22,050 --> 01:24:23,610
Great.

1138
01:24:30,760 --> 01:24:34,720
You are responsible for whatever happens to this animal, doctor.

1139
01:24:56,250 --> 01:24:58,880
I hope whatever you were really doing with my money

1140
01:24:58,960 --> 01:25:01,390
was more won'thwhile, Mr. Tesla.

1141
01:25:57,310 --> 01:25:58,300
Alley!

1142
01:26:01,390 --> 01:26:03,110
So the machine was working?

1143
01:26:03,190 --> 01:26:06,820
I never checked the calibration,'cause the hat never moved.

1144
01:26:06,890 --> 01:26:09,760
These things never quite work as you expect them to.

1145
01:26:09,830 --> 01:26:12,320
That's one of the principal beauties of science.

1146
01:26:12,400 --> 01:26:15,850
I'll need a couple of weeks to iron out the problems with the machine.

1147
01:26:15,930 --> 01:26:17,990
We'll send word when it's ready.

1148
01:26:20,200 --> 01:26:23,170
Don't forget your hat.

1149
01:26:23,240 --> 01:26:26,970
Well, which one is mine? They are all your hats.

1150
01:26:32,920 --> 01:26:35,910
You look so pretty in this new dress of yours.

1151
01:26:35,990 --> 01:26:39,820
Are we going to the zoo? No, Daddy has some errands to run.

1152
01:26:39,890 --> 01:26:42,120
But you promised. I promised, did I?

1153
01:26:42,190 --> 01:26:44,490
Then go to the zoo, we shall.

1154
01:26:44,560 --> 01:26:48,020
Daddy will go run his errands, and I'll be back before you know it.

1155
01:26:48,100 --> 01:26:51,830
So you go get ready. We'll go see those chimpanzees.

1156
01:26:54,840 --> 01:26:58,240
Sarah... what are you doing?

1157
01:27:01,910 --> 01:27:04,900
Each of us have our vices.

1158
01:27:08,020 --> 01:27:10,490
Sarah, whatever you may think...

1159
01:27:12,060 --> 01:27:15,690
...your only competition for my affections is our little girl.

1160
01:27:15,760 --> 01:27:18,730
I love you. I will always love you

1161
01:27:18,800 --> 01:27:20,230
and you alone.

1162
01:27:22,100 --> 01:27:25,900
You mean it today. Absolutely.

1163
01:27:25,970 --> 01:27:29,800
Which makes it so much harder when you don't.

1164
01:27:39,150 --> 01:27:41,950
More shopping. She does love the smell of money.

1165
01:27:42,020 --> 01:27:46,320
The little lady wants to go to the zoo, so I thought you could take her, yeah?

1166
01:27:46,390 --> 01:27:48,760
I mean, I can do it tomorrow, if not.

1167
01:27:48,830 --> 01:27:52,130
And, uh... Sarah,

1168
01:27:52,200 --> 01:27:54,320
she knows.

1169
01:27:54,400 --> 01:27:58,560
Uh, at least she knows that something is not right,

1170
01:27:58,640 --> 01:28:01,540
so if you can just do whatever you can to help me.

1171
01:28:01,610 --> 01:28:06,170
Talk to her, just convince her that I do love her.

1172
01:28:16,820 --> 01:28:18,790
What is it, Freddie?

1173
01:28:18,860 --> 01:28:21,150
Listen, don't call me that, please.

1174
01:28:21,230 --> 01:28:25,090
It's nothing. It's just that sometimes it seems wrong.

1175
01:28:25,160 --> 01:28:27,650
I told you, when you're with me, you're with me.

1176
01:28:27,730 --> 01:28:30,760
Leave your family at home where they belong.

1177
01:28:30,830 --> 01:28:34,100
I'm trying. I'm trying, Olivia.

1178
01:28:34,170 --> 01:28:36,540
I'll get dressed. Yeah.

1179
01:28:38,040 --> 01:28:41,070
I saw Fallon hanging around again.

1180
01:28:41,150 --> 01:28:43,610
There's something about that man I don't trust.

1181
01:28:43,680 --> 01:28:46,740
You trust me? Then trust Fallon.

1182
01:28:46,820 --> 01:28:49,480
He protects the things that I care about.

1183
01:29:27,720 --> 01:29:30,220
We're sorry to see you go, Mr. Angier.

1184
01:29:30,290 --> 01:29:32,850
We were sorry to see Mr. Tesla leave, as well.

1185
01:29:32,930 --> 01:29:35,020
He's very good to Colorado Springs.

1186
01:29:35,100 --> 01:29:37,760
Mr. Angier,

1187
01:29:37,830 --> 01:29:41,460
I didn't think it was necessary to tell Edisors men about the box.

1188
01:29:41,540 --> 01:29:43,200
What box?

1189
01:30:08,530 --> 01:30:11,930
I apologize for leaving without saying goodbye.

1190
01:30:12,000 --> 01:30:17,240
But I seem to have outstayed my welcome in Colorado.

1191
01:30:17,310 --> 01:30:21,800
The truly extraordinary is not permitted in science and industry.

1192
01:30:21,880 --> 01:30:24,680
Perhaps you'll find more luck in your field,

1193
01:30:24,750 --> 01:30:27,410
where people are happy to be mystified.

1194
01:30:27,480 --> 01:30:31,440
You will find what you are looking for in this box.

1195
01:30:31,520 --> 01:30:35,960
Alley has written you a thorough set of instructions.

1196
01:30:36,030 --> 01:30:40,760
I add only one suggestion on using the machine.

1197
01:30:40,830 --> 01:30:42,800
Destroy it.

1198
01:30:42,870 --> 01:30:46,170
Drop it to the bottom of the deepest ocean.

1199
01:30:46,240 --> 01:30:49,640
Such a thing will bring you only misery.

1200
01:30:53,510 --> 01:30:57,140
Tesla's warning is as uneeded as he knew it would be.

1201
01:30:57,210 --> 01:31:00,780
Today I tested the machine. Taking precautions in case

1202
01:31:00,850 --> 01:31:03,820
Tesla hadrt ironed out the kinks in its operation.

1203
01:31:03,890 --> 01:31:05,680
But if it went wrong,

1204
01:31:05,760 --> 01:31:07,920
I would not want to live like that for long.

1205
01:31:27,380 --> 01:31:31,510
But here at the turn, I must leave you, Borden.

1206
01:31:31,580 --> 01:31:33,810
Yes, you, Borden,

1207
01:31:33,880 --> 01:31:36,250
sitting there in your cell,

1208
01:31:36,320 --> 01:31:40,350
reading my diary, awaiting your death,

1209
01:31:40,420 --> 01:31:42,420
for my murder.

1210
01:31:47,760 --> 01:31:51,060
Angier's journal, that gesture of good faith, that's a fake.

1211
01:31:51,130 --> 01:31:52,430
I assure you, it's not.

1212
01:31:52,500 --> 01:31:55,440
The provenance of the journal is clear and under no doubt.

1213
01:31:55,510 --> 01:32:00,000
And it's written in Angier's own hand, for which we have numerous examples.

1214
01:32:01,880 --> 01:32:03,780
It don't matter.

1215
01:32:05,720 --> 01:32:07,740
So my tricks.

1216
01:32:10,990 --> 01:32:14,450
All of them. Including The Transported Man?

1217
01:32:15,890 --> 01:32:19,090
Lord Caldlow will be very pleased. No, he won't.

1218
01:32:19,160 --> 01:32:21,530
They're not complete.

1219
01:32:21,600 --> 01:32:24,330
It's just the pledge and the turn for each.

1220
01:32:24,400 --> 01:32:28,460
Without the prestige for these tricks, these are won'thless.

1221
01:32:28,540 --> 01:32:30,560
Yeah.

1222
01:32:30,640 --> 01:32:34,170
You get the rest of it... when you bring my daughter here.

1223
01:32:36,910 --> 01:32:39,140
I want to say goodbye.

1224
01:32:40,880 --> 01:32:44,050
We have to go through it now. We have to go through it now.

1225
01:32:44,120 --> 01:32:46,490
Now, listen to me, deny it all you want.

1226
01:32:46,560 --> 01:32:48,920
Olivia means nothing.

1227
01:32:48,990 --> 01:32:50,790
Olivia? I need an assistant.

1228
01:32:50,860 --> 01:32:53,690
I'll go to her, I'll tell her. You'll tell her what?

1229
01:32:53,760 --> 01:32:55,820
I know what you really are, Alfred.

1230
01:32:57,930 --> 01:32:59,700
I know. Sarah, Sarah, Sarah.

1231
01:32:59,770 --> 01:33:02,400
I can't keep it in. You can't talk like this.

1232
01:33:02,470 --> 01:33:05,000
No. No. Sarah, shut up! Sarah, shut up!

1233
01:33:05,080 --> 01:33:09,210
I don't want to hear it anymore. You can't talk like this.

1234
01:33:11,820 --> 01:33:15,220
You can't hide it anymore, because I know, Alfred, I know.

1235
01:33:15,290 --> 01:33:18,720
I know what you really are, Alfred.

1236
01:33:18,790 --> 01:33:21,590
I can't live like this.

1237
01:33:21,660 --> 01:33:23,790
Oh, well, you think I can live like this?

1238
01:33:23,860 --> 01:33:27,020
You think I bloody enjoy living like this?

1239
01:33:27,100 --> 01:33:31,500
We have this beautiful house, lovely little girl, we're married.

1240
01:33:31,570 --> 01:33:33,230
What is so wrong with your life?

1241
01:33:33,300 --> 01:33:35,900
Alfred, I can't live like this!

1242
01:33:35,970 --> 01:33:38,340
Well, what do you want from me?

1243
01:33:41,680 --> 01:33:45,380
I... I want...

1244
01:33:45,450 --> 01:33:48,510
I want you to be... honest with me.

1245
01:33:51,250 --> 01:33:53,720
No tricks.

1246
01:33:53,790 --> 01:33:55,880
No lies.

1247
01:33:55,960 --> 01:33:59,760
And no secrets.

1248
01:34:06,040 --> 01:34:09,370
Do you... do you love me?

1249
01:34:12,440 --> 01:34:14,070
Not today.

1250
01:34:16,210 --> 01:34:17,270
No.

1251
01:35:54,950 --> 01:35:55,970
Who's there?

1252
01:35:59,120 --> 01:36:01,210
I'm looking for an old friend.

1253
01:36:01,280 --> 01:36:04,220
I heard about a booking, nice little theater,

1254
01:36:04,290 --> 01:36:06,720
good up-and-coming magician. You're back.

1255
01:36:06,790 --> 01:36:08,380
It's good to see you, John.

1256
01:36:09,160 --> 01:36:11,890
Good rehearsal space.

1257
01:36:11,960 --> 01:36:14,730
Blind stagehands, I like it.

1258
01:36:14,800 --> 01:36:17,460
You always had a good eye for publicity.

1259
01:36:17,530 --> 01:36:18,870
I need your help, John.

1260
01:36:18,940 --> 01:36:21,730
It's my last show. A limited engagement.

1261
01:36:21,810 --> 01:36:23,430
Your last show?

1262
01:36:23,510 --> 01:36:27,640
A wise man once told me obsession was a young mars game.

1263
01:36:27,710 --> 01:36:31,270
I'm almost done. There's one thing left.

1264
01:36:32,280 --> 01:36:34,810
The Real Transported Man.

1265
01:36:36,620 --> 01:36:38,610
You want to design a show around it?

1266
01:36:38,690 --> 01:36:41,560
I don't want you backstage I need you in front managing.

1267
01:36:41,630 --> 01:36:44,290
Call in any favors or connections you have left

1268
01:36:44,360 --> 01:36:45,830
to get us the right booking.

1269
01:36:45,900 --> 01:36:47,990
What sort of booking you after?

1270
01:36:48,060 --> 01:36:50,460
The sort that Borden can't ignore.

1271
01:36:56,840 --> 01:37:00,070
Oh, what an honor it is to see you again, sir.

1272
01:37:00,140 --> 01:37:04,100
You told me you only wanted to show me one trick that'd pique my interest.

1273
01:37:04,180 --> 01:37:07,340
It's a very clever trick. Pleased to meet you, Mr. Ackerman.

1274
01:37:07,420 --> 01:37:10,280
Likewise, I'm sure. Let's get on, shall we?

1275
01:37:13,920 --> 01:37:15,690
Turn it on, gentlemen.

1276
01:37:20,930 --> 01:37:22,060
Very pretty.

1277
01:37:33,110 --> 01:37:36,770
That's it, Cutter? He simply disappears?

1278
01:37:36,850 --> 01:37:39,210
That's not a trick. He has to come back.

1279
01:37:39,280 --> 01:37:41,440
There has to be a... A prestige?

1280
01:37:41,520 --> 01:37:42,510
Exactly.

1281
01:37:45,520 --> 01:37:48,180
Pardon me.

1282
01:37:48,260 --> 01:37:51,520
It's very rare to see...

1283
01:37:51,590 --> 01:37:53,060
...real magic.

1284
01:37:54,560 --> 01:37:56,260
It's been many years since...

1285
01:37:56,330 --> 01:37:58,060
Are you interested in helping us?

1286
01:38:00,140 --> 01:38:03,870
Yes... but you'll have to dress it up a little.

1287
01:38:06,140 --> 01:38:07,800
Disguise it.

1288
01:38:09,480 --> 01:38:11,970
Give them enough reason to doubt it.

1289
01:38:12,780 --> 01:38:16,010
You haven't spoken about her, Freddie, not once.

1290
01:38:16,090 --> 01:38:19,780
Why would I talk about her to you, hm?

1291
01:38:19,860 --> 01:38:23,720
Because she was a part of your life and now she's gone.

1292
01:38:26,700 --> 01:38:31,190
You know, the day before she killed herself she said she wanted to meet me.

1293
01:38:31,270 --> 01:38:34,200
That she had something to tell me about you.

1294
01:38:34,270 --> 01:38:37,870
I was such a coward, I couldn't bring myself to face her.

1295
01:38:37,940 --> 01:38:39,430
But...

1296
01:38:40,640 --> 01:38:42,910
...what would she have said?

1297
01:38:42,980 --> 01:38:46,070
You want to know the truth about me?

1298
01:38:47,350 --> 01:38:49,840
The truth is that...

1299
01:38:52,020 --> 01:38:54,010
...I never loved Sarah.

1300
01:38:55,230 --> 01:38:56,720
I never loved her.

1301
01:38:56,790 --> 01:38:59,020
You married her, you had a child with her.

1302
01:38:59,100 --> 01:39:02,190
Yes. Part of me. Part of me did, but...

1303
01:39:02,270 --> 01:39:04,200
...the other part didn't.

1304
01:39:04,270 --> 01:39:08,500
The part that found you, the part that's sitting here right now.

1305
01:39:08,570 --> 01:39:11,630
I love you. No, Olivia, I love you.

1306
01:39:11,710 --> 01:39:13,680
That's the truth.

1307
01:39:13,740 --> 01:39:16,140
That is the truth that matters.

1308
01:39:16,210 --> 01:39:20,620
You could be in some other restaurant with some other woman right now,

1309
01:39:20,680 --> 01:39:22,280
talking about me that way.

1310
01:39:22,350 --> 01:39:24,480
No. Yes.

1311
01:39:27,920 --> 01:39:31,660
It's inuman to be so cold.

1312
01:39:37,330 --> 01:39:38,600
He's back.

1313
01:39:39,500 --> 01:39:42,770
After two years, he's got a new trick.

1314
01:39:42,840 --> 01:39:45,500
They're saying it's the best Londors ever seen.

1315
01:39:47,710 --> 01:39:50,180
You should see the look on your face, Professor.

1316
01:39:52,050 --> 01:39:53,980
You should go to him.

1317
01:39:54,050 --> 01:39:56,580
You two deserve each other.

1318
01:40:01,560 --> 01:40:05,490
But I'm afraid I'm booked. The Moscow Ballet plays through next year.

1319
01:40:05,560 --> 01:40:10,120
Then get rid of them. There will be 100 performances, no more, no less.

1320
01:40:10,200 --> 01:40:12,690
Five performances a week, no matinees.

1321
01:40:14,500 --> 01:40:17,530
That's what you'll charge for each ticket. Good day.

1322
01:40:40,260 --> 01:40:44,890
Ladies and gentlemen, my first trick of the evening involves considerable risk.

1323
01:40:46,140 --> 01:40:47,500
Anyone in the audience

1324
01:40:47,570 --> 01:40:50,400
who would be irrevocably damaged by seeing a man drown

1325
01:40:50,470 --> 01:40:52,070
should leave now.

1326
01:40:52,140 --> 01:40:54,940
When I tell you the lady who taught me this illusion

1327
01:40:55,010 --> 01:40:57,000
actually died performing it,

1328
01:40:57,080 --> 01:41:00,380
you'll understand the seriousness of the dangers involved.

1329
01:41:02,550 --> 01:41:04,950
Let's begin.

1330
01:41:14,660 --> 01:41:17,290
In my travels, I have seen the future.

1331
01:41:19,100 --> 01:41:22,700
And it is a strange future, indeed.

1332
01:41:22,770 --> 01:41:24,930
The world, ladies and gentlemen,

1333
01:41:25,010 --> 01:41:29,110
is on the brink of new and terrifying possibilities.

1334
01:41:49,600 --> 01:41:53,060
What you're about to witness is not magic.

1335
01:41:54,240 --> 01:41:55,640
It's purely science.

1336
01:41:55,710 --> 01:41:58,140
I'd like to invite you to come up on stage now

1337
01:41:58,210 --> 01:42:01,200
so that you can examine the machine for yourselves.

1338
01:42:56,500 --> 01:42:58,330
Mars reach

1339
01:42:58,400 --> 01:43:01,530
exceeds his imagination.

1340
01:43:12,820 --> 01:43:14,180
Bravo!

1341
01:43:22,230 --> 01:43:24,960
One hundred performances. Why?

1342
01:43:25,030 --> 01:43:26,590
Does his method dictate that?

1343
01:43:26,660 --> 01:43:28,560
Is it a publicity move? What is it?

1344
01:43:28,630 --> 01:43:31,000
He's a no-talent magician, and they call him

1345
01:43:31,070 --> 01:43:33,470
the bloody best in England. Why?

1346
01:43:33,540 --> 01:43:36,300
Fifty yards in a second! In a second!

1347
01:43:36,370 --> 01:43:39,470
And all that we know is he uses a trap door.

1348
01:43:39,540 --> 01:43:43,810
Brilliant! What is going on under that stage? Huh?!

1349
01:43:43,880 --> 01:43:46,310
Why can't you out-think him?

1350
01:44:01,430 --> 01:44:06,630
They do this every night, after each performance. Yeah?

1351
01:44:32,930 --> 01:44:34,680
Huh.

1352
01:44:35,130 --> 01:44:38,100
We're done.

1353
01:44:39,400 --> 01:44:40,960
All right?

1354
01:44:42,270 --> 01:44:44,030
Let him have his trick.

1355
01:44:44,110 --> 01:44:46,600
I don't need his secret.

1356
01:44:46,680 --> 01:44:48,170
So...

1357
01:44:49,680 --> 01:44:52,650
...don't go back there, you leave him alone.

1358
01:44:52,720 --> 01:44:55,980
Both of us... just leave him alone.

1359
01:44:56,050 --> 01:44:57,680
We're done.

1360
01:45:06,430 --> 01:45:08,490
I told you, John.

1361
01:45:08,560 --> 01:45:10,660
I don't want you backstage on this one.

1362
01:45:12,300 --> 01:45:14,700
I would like to invite you onstage now

1363
01:45:14,770 --> 01:45:17,470
so that you can examine the machine for yourselves.

1364
01:45:46,670 --> 01:45:50,400
Hey, where do you think? I'm part of the bloody act, you fool.

1365
01:45:59,420 --> 01:46:00,400
Who was that?

1366
01:46:56,970 --> 01:47:01,100
Hey! Where's the bloody key?!

1367
01:47:01,180 --> 01:47:02,740
Where's the bloody key?!

1368
01:47:05,180 --> 01:47:08,910
Where's the bloody key?! He's bloody drowning!

1369
01:47:18,160 --> 01:47:19,150
Hold on!

1370
01:47:24,470 --> 01:47:25,930
What have you done?

1371
01:47:47,760 --> 01:47:53,200
Alfred Borden, you have been found guilty of the murder of Robert Angier.

1372
01:47:53,260 --> 01:47:57,490
You will be hanged by the neck until dead.

1373
01:47:57,570 --> 01:48:00,540
May the Lord have mercy on your soul.

1374
01:48:02,310 --> 01:48:04,470
Mr. Cutter?

1375
01:48:04,540 --> 01:48:06,170
Owens.

1376
01:48:06,240 --> 01:48:09,080
Oh, thank you for coming, Mr. Owens.

1377
01:48:09,150 --> 01:48:13,510
It has fallen to me to dispose of Mr. Angier's equipment.

1378
01:48:13,580 --> 01:48:15,170
But I notice from this manifest

1379
01:48:15,250 --> 01:48:18,950
that Lord Caldlow has purchased the bulk of the items.

1380
01:48:19,020 --> 01:48:22,320
Mr. Cutter, if you need to know where to deliver these items...

1381
01:48:22,390 --> 01:48:25,990
No, it's just there is one particular item,

1382
01:48:26,930 --> 01:48:29,520
this item, in fact,

1383
01:48:29,600 --> 01:48:31,860
I would like to... You would like to...

1384
01:48:31,930 --> 01:48:33,030
...buy.

1385
01:48:33,100 --> 01:48:35,000
Buy yourself? Yeah, I suppose so.

1386
01:48:36,240 --> 01:48:38,170
This is the machine? Yes.

1387
01:48:38,240 --> 01:48:40,470
Well, I'm afraid Lord Caldlow was adamant

1388
01:48:40,540 --> 01:48:42,770
about purchasing this particular item.

1389
01:48:42,850 --> 01:48:45,940
Do you think I could talk to Lord Caldlow in person?

1390
01:48:46,020 --> 01:48:48,780
Out of the question, I'm afraid.

1391
01:48:51,220 --> 01:48:53,120
Of course,

1392
01:48:53,190 --> 01:48:56,420
I suppose if, in the course of your delivery arrangements,

1393
01:48:56,490 --> 01:49:00,950
your paths were to cross, I can't stop you speaking your mind.

1394
01:49:01,930 --> 01:49:03,060
Thank you.

1395
01:49:04,630 --> 01:49:06,790
Still here, Borden? For now.

1396
01:49:07,400 --> 01:49:10,370
You got a visitor. Lord Caldlow.

1397
01:49:10,440 --> 01:49:12,530
With a little girl.

1398
01:49:20,820 --> 01:49:22,720
Jess?

1399
01:49:22,790 --> 01:49:24,410
Hello, my love.

1400
01:49:24,490 --> 01:49:27,950
How are you? I've missed you so much.

1401
01:49:28,020 --> 01:49:30,150
Fallors missed you too. We both have.

1402
01:49:30,230 --> 01:49:32,560
Daddy, can I come in there?

1403
01:49:33,200 --> 01:49:35,820
Not right now. Not right now, darling.

1404
01:49:35,900 --> 01:49:38,160
No, but everything is gonna be all right.

1405
01:49:39,740 --> 01:49:41,460
You must be Lord Caldlow?

1406
01:49:41,540 --> 01:49:45,270
Caldlow. Yes, I am. I always have been.

1407
01:49:50,310 --> 01:49:52,510
They flatter you with all those chains.

1408
01:49:52,580 --> 01:49:55,610
Don't they know you can't escape without your rubber ball?

1409
01:49:55,680 --> 01:49:58,680
I pulled you out... out of that tank.

1410
01:49:58,750 --> 01:50:02,590
All I wanted to do was prove that I was a better magician.

1411
01:50:02,660 --> 01:50:04,560
But you couldn't leave me alone.

1412
01:50:06,060 --> 01:50:08,150
I don't know what you've done.

1413
01:50:10,070 --> 01:50:13,760
But you're not afraid to get your hands dirty anymore, are you?

1414
01:50:13,840 --> 01:50:17,100
No, not anymore. And I win.

1415
01:50:17,170 --> 01:50:20,510
No one cares about the man in the box, the man who disappears.

1416
01:50:20,580 --> 01:50:21,700
You win?

1417
01:50:23,150 --> 01:50:27,810
This ain't a bloody competition anymore. This is my little girl's life.

1418
01:50:27,880 --> 01:50:30,880
And don't you dare put her in the middle of this.

1419
01:50:30,950 --> 01:50:33,950
Oh, I know how hard it is to have someone so special

1420
01:50:34,020 --> 01:50:36,390
taken away from you, don't I, Borden?

1421
01:50:37,390 --> 01:50:40,120
And you can't take it with you, now, can you?

1422
01:50:40,200 --> 01:50:42,100
Oh, she'll be looked after.

1423
01:50:42,160 --> 01:50:44,190
Goodbye, Professor. Come on, darling.

1424
01:50:44,270 --> 01:50:46,030
No, no, no, no, stop, my darling.

1425
01:50:46,100 --> 01:50:48,230
Look, here.

1426
01:50:49,770 --> 01:50:53,040
That's what you're after. That's what this is about.

1427
01:50:53,840 --> 01:50:55,210
Take it.

1428
01:50:55,280 --> 01:50:56,970
Your secret? Yeah.

1429
01:51:00,180 --> 01:51:03,740
You always were the better magician. We both know that.

1430
01:51:04,390 --> 01:51:06,980
Whatever your secret was, you have to agree,

1431
01:51:08,120 --> 01:51:09,450
mine is better.

1432
01:51:11,160 --> 01:51:14,530
Don't do this. Don't do this, Angier. Jess, Jess!

1433
01:51:14,600 --> 01:51:18,120
Jess, I'm gonna take you home soon. I promise.

1434
01:51:18,200 --> 01:51:20,760
For God's sake. I promise. Look!

1435
01:51:24,610 --> 01:51:28,200
I promise. I love you, Jess.

1436
01:51:28,280 --> 01:51:29,940
I love you.

1437
01:51:30,010 --> 01:51:31,640
Come on. I love you.

1438
01:51:31,710 --> 01:51:33,410
I love you, Jess.

1439
01:51:33,480 --> 01:51:35,810
Angier! Angier!

1440
01:51:35,890 --> 01:51:37,980
What, do you think this place can hold me?

1441
01:51:38,050 --> 01:51:41,080
Angier! They're gonna bloody hang me!

1442
01:51:41,160 --> 01:51:43,680
And you can put a stop to this now!

1443
01:51:44,330 --> 01:51:46,520
Listen! Listen! That man...

1444
01:51:46,600 --> 01:51:48,960
Listen, that man is the one I'm in for killing!

1445
01:51:49,030 --> 01:51:53,370
Listen! If he's alive, I'm not guilty!

1446
01:51:53,440 --> 01:51:55,700
I'm not guilty! I don't care!

1447
01:51:55,770 --> 01:51:57,860
Get hold of him. Angier!

1448
01:52:02,550 --> 01:52:05,110
Run along now.

1449
01:52:05,180 --> 01:52:07,710
Sir, there's a gentleman waiting.

1450
01:52:24,530 --> 01:52:27,260
Dear God. Hello, Cutter.

1451
01:52:28,240 --> 01:52:30,670
You're still alive.

1452
01:52:30,740 --> 01:52:32,600
How is it you're still alive?

1453
01:52:32,680 --> 01:52:35,080
I saw you on a slab, for God's sake.

1454
01:52:38,080 --> 01:52:40,110
Child. Good night, sir.

1455
01:52:40,180 --> 01:52:42,150
Good night, Jess.

1456
01:52:43,390 --> 01:52:45,050
I've seen her before.

1457
01:52:45,120 --> 01:52:47,650
I saw her in court with...

1458
01:52:48,590 --> 01:52:50,220
...Fallon.

1459
01:52:52,330 --> 01:52:54,920
What have you done? She needs looking after.

1460
01:52:55,000 --> 01:52:57,490
She needs her father.

1461
01:52:57,570 --> 01:53:00,540
You're letting him hang, and I helped you.

1462
01:53:00,600 --> 01:53:05,540
I came here to beg Lord Caldlow to let me destroy that machine.

1463
01:53:07,010 --> 01:53:10,450
I am not going to beg you for anything.

1464
01:53:10,510 --> 01:53:13,780
You don't have to. I'm gonna make sure it is never used again.

1465
01:53:14,920 --> 01:53:18,010
Then, Lord Caldlow...

1466
01:53:20,560 --> 01:53:22,620
...where do you want me to deliver it?

1467
01:53:22,690 --> 01:53:26,460
My theater. It belongs with the prestige materials.

1468
01:53:26,930 --> 01:53:29,520
John, I tried not to involve you.

1469
01:53:42,480 --> 01:53:44,740
So...

1470
01:53:46,780 --> 01:53:49,380
We go alone now.

1471
01:53:49,450 --> 01:53:51,890
Both of us.

1472
01:53:54,690 --> 01:53:58,090
I mean, I don't have as far to go as you.

1473
01:54:03,400 --> 01:54:04,730
No.

1474
01:54:06,270 --> 01:54:09,330
You were right. I should have left him to his damn trick.

1475
01:54:11,110 --> 01:54:13,200
I'm sorry.

1476
01:54:14,740 --> 01:54:17,080
I'm sorry for a lot of things.

1477
01:54:18,210 --> 01:54:20,940
I'm sorry about Sarah.

1478
01:54:22,050 --> 01:54:25,510
Didn't mean to hurt her, I didn't.

1479
01:54:27,390 --> 01:54:31,720
You go live your life in full now, all right? You live for both of us.

1480
01:54:40,570 --> 01:54:42,060
Goodbye.

1481
01:55:27,850 --> 01:55:30,280
Are you watching closely?

1482
01:56:01,650 --> 01:56:03,580
Put it down on the end.

1483
01:56:36,990 --> 01:56:40,750
Take a minute to consider your achievement.

1484
01:56:41,820 --> 01:56:44,620
I once told you about a sailor

1485
01:56:44,690 --> 01:56:46,990
who described drowning to me.

1486
01:56:47,060 --> 01:56:49,260
Yes, he said it was like going home.

1487
01:56:50,370 --> 01:56:54,130
I was lying. He said it was agony.

1488
01:57:33,880 --> 01:57:38,040
Alfred Borden, this day, in the name of the king

1489
01:57:38,110 --> 01:57:40,100
and the High Court of England,

1490
01:57:40,180 --> 01:57:42,010
you will meet your end.

1491
01:57:44,120 --> 01:57:46,140
No one cares about the man in the box.

1492
01:57:50,990 --> 01:57:52,720
Cutter?

1493
01:57:57,400 --> 01:57:58,920
Cutter!

1494
01:58:00,800 --> 01:58:03,070
Do you have anything to say?

1495
01:58:07,810 --> 01:58:09,210
咒语

1496
01:58:45,850 --> 01:58:48,580
A brother? A twin?

1497
01:58:50,420 --> 01:58:52,790
You were Fallon.

1498
01:58:54,290 --> 01:58:56,950
The whole time? No.

1499
01:58:57,990 --> 01:59:00,330
We were both Fallon.

1500
01:59:00,400 --> 01:59:02,300
We were both Borden.

1501
01:59:07,670 --> 01:59:10,640
Were you the one who went into the box?

1502
01:59:10,710 --> 01:59:12,400
Or the one who came back out?

1503
01:59:13,710 --> 01:59:16,010
We took turns.

1504
01:59:20,820 --> 01:59:22,880
The trick is where we would swap.

1505
01:59:29,090 --> 01:59:30,990
Cutter knew. Cutter knew.

1506
01:59:31,060 --> 01:59:33,320
But I told him it was too simple, too easy.

1507
01:59:34,530 --> 01:59:35,520
No.

1508
01:59:37,070 --> 01:59:39,030
Simple, maybe, but not easy.

1509
01:59:39,100 --> 01:59:42,870
There's nothing easy about two men sharing one life.

1510
01:59:48,540 --> 01:59:51,740
I don't understand, how it can be bleeding again?

1511
01:59:55,220 --> 01:59:58,020
Wha... What about Olivia and your wife?

1512
01:59:59,020 --> 02:00:00,920
We each loved one of them.

1513
02:00:04,560 --> 02:00:07,290
I loved Sarah.

1514
02:00:07,360 --> 02:00:09,890
He loved Olivia.

1515
02:00:09,970 --> 02:00:12,400
We each had half of a full life,

1516
02:00:12,470 --> 02:00:15,460
really, which was enough for us...

1517
02:00:15,540 --> 02:00:16,530
...just.

1518
02:00:19,170 --> 02:00:20,900
But not for them.

1519
02:00:23,480 --> 02:00:25,710
See, sacrifice, Robert,

1520
02:00:25,780 --> 02:00:28,510
that's the price of a good trick.

1521
02:00:30,550 --> 02:00:33,110
You wouldn't know anything about that, would you?

1522
02:00:36,390 --> 02:00:38,330
I've... I've made sacrifices.

1523
02:00:38,390 --> 02:00:40,360
No. Yes.

1524
02:00:40,830 --> 02:00:43,920
It takes nothing to steal another mars work.

1525
02:00:44,000 --> 02:00:46,760
Yeah, it takes everything.

1526
02:01:06,820 --> 02:01:08,950
No, no, wait, I'm not...

1527
02:01:15,630 --> 02:01:18,260
It took courage.

1528
02:01:20,270 --> 02:01:23,470
It took courage to climb into that machine every night,

1529
02:01:24,270 --> 02:01:26,710
not knowing...

1530
02:01:26,780 --> 02:01:28,900
...if I'd be the man in the box...

1531
02:01:31,950 --> 02:01:33,210
...or in the prestige.

1532
02:01:34,550 --> 02:01:37,580
Do you want to see...

1533
02:01:37,650 --> 02:01:39,950
...what it cost me?

1534
02:01:40,020 --> 02:01:42,790
You didn't see where you are, did you?

1535
02:01:43,690 --> 02:01:46,090
Look. Look.

1536
02:01:47,200 --> 02:01:48,690
I don't care.

1537
02:01:53,400 --> 02:01:56,960
You went halfway around the world.

1538
02:01:57,040 --> 02:01:59,800
You spent a fortune.

1539
02:02:01,880 --> 02:02:04,400
You did terrible things.

1540
02:02:05,410 --> 02:02:07,880
Really terrible things, Robert.

1541
02:02:09,920 --> 02:02:11,410
And all for nothing.

1542
02:02:12,390 --> 02:02:13,580
For nothing?

1543
02:02:13,660 --> 02:02:14,990
Yeah.

1544
02:02:18,660 --> 02:02:21,290
You never understood...

1545
02:02:21,360 --> 02:02:23,350
...why we did this.

1546
02:02:26,470 --> 02:02:29,030
The audience knows the truth.

1547
02:02:30,170 --> 02:02:32,610
The world is simple.

1548
02:02:32,670 --> 02:02:34,400
It's miserable.

1549
02:02:34,480 --> 02:02:38,170
Solid... Solid all the way through.

1550
02:02:40,520 --> 02:02:42,850
But if you could fool them,

1551
02:02:42,920 --> 02:02:45,080
even for a second...

1552
02:02:46,620 --> 02:02:49,020
...then you could make them wonder.

1553
02:02:50,160 --> 02:02:52,350
And then you...

1554
02:02:52,430 --> 02:02:54,660
Then you got to see something

1555
02:02:54,730 --> 02:02:56,390
very special.

1556
02:03:00,700 --> 02:03:03,230
You really don't know.

1557
02:03:06,310 --> 02:03:07,970
It was...

1558
02:03:09,040 --> 02:03:12,010
...it was the look on their faces.

1559
02:03:27,200 --> 02:03:32,030
Every magic trick consists of three parts, or acts.

1560
02:03:35,470 --> 02:03:38,930
The first part is called "the pledge."

1561
02:03:39,010 --> 02:03:41,770
The magician shows you something ordinary.

1562
02:03:42,910 --> 02:03:45,850
The second act is called "the turn."

1563
02:03:47,320 --> 02:03:50,180
The magician takes the ordinary something

1564
02:03:50,250 --> 02:03:53,120
and makes it into something extraordinary.

1565
02:03:54,460 --> 02:03:58,360
But you wouldn't clap yet, because making something disappear

1566
02:03:58,430 --> 02:04:02,520
isn't enough. You have to bring it back.

1567
02:04:36,970 --> 02:04:39,400
Now you're looking for the secret.

1568
02:04:42,340 --> 02:04:46,070
But you won't find it, because, of course, you're not really looking.

1569
02:04:49,180 --> 02:04:52,610
You don't really want to work it out.

1570
02:04:55,420 --> 02:04:57,720
You want to be fooled.

